Long: Intermittent 409 Conflict when two users save the same profile concurrently. Example stack trace:
org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException: could not execute statement; SQL [n/a]; constraint [UK_profile_user];
	at org.hibernate.exception.ConstraintViolationException: could not execute statement
Caused by: java.sql.SQLIntegrityConstraintViolationException: Duplicate entry 'user123' for key 'UK_profile_user'

Current controller flow:
@PostMapping("/api/profiles")
public ResponseEntity<Profile> save(@RequestBody Profile p) {
  Profile existing = profileRepo.findByUserId(p.getUserId());
  if (existing == null) {
    return ResponseEntity.ok(profileRepo.save(p));
  }
  existing.merge(p);
  return ResponseEntity.ok(profileRepo.save(existing));
}

This suffers from a race between find and save. Proposed fix: add unique constraint handling with retry on Transactional propagation REQUIRED_NEW, or use repository.saveAndFlush and catch DataIntegrityViolationException to fetch existing and return 409 with latest profile. Add integration test simulating concurrent requests with CompletableFuture to reproduce.

Long: API consumers report that GET /api/transactions returns inconsistent timezone offsets and clients in UTC see wrong ordering. Here is an example response:
[
  {"id":1, "timestamp":"2025-11-10T10:00:00-05:00"},
  {"id":2, "timestamp":"2025-11-10T16:00:00+01:00"}
]
Sorting is done in Java with Date objects but deserialization to OffsetDateTime varies depending on Jackson settings. Logs show:
2025-11-10 12:02:10 INFO TransactionService - Retrieved 2 transactions, ordering unstable

Fix: normalize all stored timestamps to UTC (Instant) in DB (TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE) and in DTOs serialize using Instant.toString() or OffsetDateTime.withOffsetSameInstant(ZoneOffset.UTC). Update repository queries to sort by timestamp in DB (ORDER BY created_at) rather than in-memory. Include migration script to convert existing records and update JPA mapping:

@Column(name = "created_at")
private Instant createdAt;

Add unit tests to ensure order is deterministic regardless of client timezone.

Long: POST /api/login returns 200 OK but cookies are not set over HTTPS in production only. Browser console shows cookie omitted. Relevant code in AuthController:
@PostMapping("/api/login")
public ResponseEntity<?> login(@RequestBody LoginDto dto, HttpServletResponse resp) {
  Session s = authService.createSession(dto);
  Cookie cookie = new Cookie("SESSIONID", s.getToken());
  cookie.setHttpOnly(true);
  cookie.setPath("/");
  resp.addCookie(cookie);
  return ResponseEntity.ok(Map.of("user", s.getUser()));
}

In production fronting load balancer terminates TLS and forwards to app over HTTP. Browsers require 'Secure' attribute to be set for cookies only when using https; but since LB uses https externally, set cookie as Secure=true and also ensure SameSite=None for cross-site requests. Update code:
cookie.setSecure(true);
cookie.setSameSite("None");

Also ensure server.use-forward-headers=true so app knows it's behind proxy. Add integration testing with staging behind LB.

Long: We are seeing performance regressions on /api/report/generate where the controller blocks for tens of seconds under concurrent load. Flame graph points to synchronous DB calls and large in-memory buffering. Code snippet:
@GetMapping("/api/report/generate")
public ResponseEntity<StreamingResponseBody> report(
    @RequestParam long from, @RequestParam long to) {
  List<Record> records = reportService.fetchRecords(from, to); // fetches millions
  StreamingResponseBody body = out -> {
    for (Record r : records) {
      out.write(formatCsv(r).getBytes());
    }
  };
  return ResponseEntity.ok()
      .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=report.csv")
      .body(body);
}

Problems: fetchRecords loads all into memory. Fix: change reportService.fetchRecords to return a Stream or use jdbcTemplate.query with RowCallbackHandler to stream results directly to OutputStream, use @Transactional(readOnly=true) with fetchSize and forward-only ResultSet for MySQL (use useCursorFetch=true). Also add backpressure mitigation and limit concurrent exports with semaphore. Add load test reproducer using JMeter.

Long: Several endpoints are throwing Jackson infinite recursion when serializing JPA entities with bidirectional relationships. Error seen in logs:
2025-11-09 15:12:34 ERROR o.s.b.w.s.c.RestExceptionHandler - JSON serialization failed
com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonMappingException: Infinite recursion (StackOverflowError)
	at com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ser.BeanSerializerFactory.createSerializer(BeanSerializerFactory.java:...)
Caused by: java.lang.StackOverflowError

Entity snippet:
@Entity
public class User {
  @OneToMany(mappedBy = "owner")
  private List<Project> projects;
}
@Entity
public class Project {
  @ManyToOne
  private User owner;
}

Current DTOs return entities directly from controller. Fix: introduce DTOs (UserDto, ProjectDto) and convert using MapStruct or manual mapper; alternatively annotate with @JsonIgnore on one side or use @JsonManagedReference/@JsonBackReference. Prefer DTO approach as best practice. Please refactor UserController to return DTOs and add tests ensuring no recursion. Also update OpenAPI docs to show DTO schemas.

Long: Database schema change needed: add column 'last_api_access' to users table and update JPA mapping. Migration SQL:
