Long: We need to change the API contract for POST /api/checkout to be asynchronous. Current behavior blocks until payment completes and sometimes times out. New flow: accept order, return 202 with Location header pointing to /api/orders/{id}/status, process payment asynchronously via background worker and publish events. Steps:
- Modify controller to persist Order with status PENDING and return 202 with Location
- Add OrderProcessor component (@Async or use a message queue) to perform payment and update status to COMPLETED or FAILED
- Create GET /api/orders/{id}/status to return {"status":"PENDING|COMPLETED|FAILED","failureReason":null}

Example controller change:
@PostMapping("/api/checkout")
public ResponseEntity<Void> checkout(@RequestBody CheckoutDto dto) {
  Order o = orderService.createPendingOrder(dto);
  orderProcessor.process(o.getId());
  URI uri = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest().path("/orders/{id}/status").buildAndExpand(o.getId()).toUri();
  return ResponseEntity.accepted().location(uri).build();
}

Also update clients to poll status or accept webhooks. Provide migration note and deprecation period for synchronous behavior.

Long: Tests failing after changing API to return Optional fields for address. Example failure:
java.lang.IllegalStateException: No value present
	at java.util.Optional.get(Optional.java:135)
	at com.example.dto.AddressDtoMapper.map(AddressDtoMapper.java:42)

Problem: Controllers still assume non-null Address returned from userService.getAddress(userId). Fix: update controllers to handle Optional properly and return 204 No Content when address is absent, or include 'address': null in JSON response per API decision. Update mapper code:
public AddressDto map(Optional<Address> opt) {
  return opt.map(a -> new AddressDto(a.getLine1(), ...)).orElse(null);
}

Also add unit tests for absent address case and update OpenAPI to mark address as nullable.

Long: Security scan flagged that API exposes stack traces in error responses when exceptions bubble up. Example returned body during 500:
{"timestamp":"...","status":500,"error":"Internal Server Error","message":"org.springframework.jdbc.BadSqlGrammarException:...","trace":"com.example.Service.method(Service.java:45)\n..."}

Fix: centralize exception handling with @ControllerAdvice and return sanitized error responses. Example:
@ControllerAdvice
public class RestExceptionHandler {
  @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
  public ResponseEntity<ErrorDto> handle(Exception e) {
    log.error("Unhandled error", e);
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
      .body(new ErrorDto("internal_error","An unexpected error occurred"));
  }
}

Also ensure stack traces are only logged server-side and that actuator/trace endpoints are secured. Add tests verifying no stack traces in body even when stacktrace enabled in dev.

Long: We need to support transactional retries on transient DB errors for API operations that perform complex multi-row updates. Example transient exception:
org.postgresql.util.PSQLException: ERROR: deadlock detected
	at org.postgresql.core.v3.QueryExecutorImpl.receiveErrorResponse(QueryExecutorImpl.java:...)

Proposal: implement a RetryTemplate around service methods that catch SQLException and check SQLState or vendor-specific codes for deadlock/timeout and retry up to 3 times with exponential backoff. Use Spring's @Transactional(propagation=REQUIRES_NEW) for retried attempts to isolate transactions. Provide sample wrapper:

public <T> T withRetry(Supplier<T> action) {
  int attempts = 0;
  while (true) {
    try { return action.get(); }
    catch (TransientDataAccessException e) {
      attempts++; if (attempts>3) throw e;
      Thread.sleep(100 * attempts);
    }
  }
}

Also add monitoring metrics for retry counts and a circuit-breaker if retries spike.

Long: GraphQL mutations for creating orders are failing with 400 when nested customer input provided. Request logs:

ERROR: GraphQLError: Variable '$input' expected value of type 'OrderInput!' but got: {customer: {email: 'a@b.com'}}

Our mutation expects ID for existing customer but we want to accept nested 'createCustomer' input to create new customer and associate. Current schema snippet:

type Mutation {
  createOrder(input: OrderInput!): Order
}

input OrderInput {
  customerId: ID
  items: [OrderItemInput!]
}

Proposal: extend schema to allow union input with customerCreate: CustomerCreateInput and validate on server. Provide resolver pseudo-code that within a single DB transaction either looks up customer by ID or creates new one, validating uniqueness on email. Add tests for both flows. Include sample mutation payload for nested create.

Long: Intermittent crash in the HTML renderer when building dynamic menus. Repro steps:
1) Send a POST /api/v1/menu with JSON payload containing menu items with `id`, `label`, and `children` arrays.
2) If `children` contains a null entry, service crashes.

Stack trace from systemd journal:

terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::bad_alloc'
  what(): std::bad_alloc
  #0 0x7f8c2b1a in __cxa_throw
  #1 0x55e4a3b2 in ui::MenuBuilder::build(std::vector<MenuItem>)
  #2 0x55e4a1f0 in api::MenuHandler::handle(http::Request const&)

Snippet from MenuBuilder:

std::string MenuBuilder::build(const std::vector<MenuItem>& items) {
    std::string html;
    for (auto &it : items) {
        html += "<li>" + it.label + "";
        if (!it.children.empty()) html += build(it.children); // recursive
        html += "</li>";
    }
    return "<ul>" + html + "</ul>";
}

We should harden against null child entries, validate payloads, and change recursion to iterative or depth-check to avoid stack/heap issues. Please add tests and proper 400 return if payload invalid.

Long: Users report flicker when switching themes because the SPA reloads all assets. Proposed change: implement server-side toggle API and a streaming SSE/WS event to notify clients, plus endpoint for retrieving CSS tokens per theme.

Current CSS token endpoint (C++ using cpp-httplib):

#include <httplib.h>

void registerRoutes(Server &sv) {
    sv.Get("/api/v1/theme/tokens", [](const Request& req, Response& res){
        auto theme = req.get_param_value("theme");
        json j = loadThemeJson(theme);
        res.set_content(j.dump(), "application/json");
    });
}

Problems:
- theme param is optional and defaults to empty -> returns 500 when string empty
- no caching headers
- no ETag

Please: validate `theme`, return 400 for unknown themes, add Cache-Control: public, max-age=900 and ETag computation. Also add an SSE endpoint /stream/theme-updates so clients can patch CSS variables without full reload. Include unit tests for token loading and invalid theme handling.

Long: Repro steps for intermittent UI freeze during heavy write loads: when many clients post to /api/v1/items, the microservice blocks event loop and the webshell (navigation overlay) becomes unresponsive.

Logs:
[2025-09-10T12:01:02Z] WARN api:db_queue_latency=1234ms
[2025-09-10T12:01:02Z] ERROR http:request_timeout id=abcd path=/api/v1/items status=504

Snippet of problematic code (pseudo-C++):

void ItemsHandler::post(const Request& req, Response& res) {
    auto body = req.body;
    // heavy synchronous DB operation
    db.insertMany(parse(body)); // can block for seconds
    res.set_status(201);
}

We should make DB writes async (use std::async or threadpool), return 202 with location/polling token for completion, and add a backpressure mechanism. Also add UI-friendly response indicating in-progress state so the client can show proper progress and avoid blocking UI.

Long: Crash when uploading SVG avatars: server throws XML parsing exceptions and returns 500. Error excerpt:

[error] xml: parser error: entity 'nbsp' not defined at line 23
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'
  what(): xml parsing failed

Relevant code in AvatarService:

std::string AvatarService::transform(const std::string &svg) {
    pugi::xml_document doc;
    pugi::xml_parse_result res = doc.load_string(svg.c_str());
    if (!res) throw std::runtime_error("xml parsing failed");
    // sanitize and rasterize
}

Problems:
- Unhandled exceptions leak to clients
- No SVG sanitization for entities or scripts

Request:
- Catch xml errors and return 400 with friendly message and validation errors array
- Use a stricter sanitizer (strip scripts, external entities)
- Provide a fallback avatar URL in the response so UI doesn't show broken image
- Add sample unit tests that feed malformed SVG strings and assert 400 responses.

Long: The API for updating user preferences is inconsistent and causes UI to lose some toggles. Example failing request and response:

Request:
PUT /api/v1/user/123/preferences
Body: { "compact_mode": true, "notify": { "email": false } }

Response returned:
{ "preferences": { "compactMode": false, "notify": { "email": false, "sms": true } } }

It seems the C++ handler is merging defaults incorrectly and using snake_case <-> camelCase mismatch. Handler code (simplified):

auto body = nlohmann::json::parse(req.body);
UserPrefs prefs = loadUserPrefs(userId);
if (body.contains("compact_mode")) prefs.compactMode = body["compact_mode"].get<bool>();
if (body.contains("notify")) prefs.notify = body["notify"].get<NotifyPrefs>();
saveUserPrefs(prefs);
res.set_content(serialize(prefs));

Problems:
- Partial updates should merge nested objects rather than overwrite defaults
- Need consistent naming and mapping layer between JSON keys and struct members

Please implement a patch that performs deep-merge of incoming JSON into existing prefs, add mapping utility for snake_case->camelCase, and add integration tests that submit partial updates and assert other fields are preserved.

Long: Timing issue when using WebSocket-based notifications causes client to show duplicate toasts. Repro:
1) Client opens WS to /ws/notify and also polls /api/v1/notifications every 10s.
2) A new notification arrives, server pushes via WS, but poll returns same notification before client marks it seen.

Server pub/sub code (C++ using Boost Asio):

void NotificationService::publish(const Notification &n) {
    store.save(n);
    for (auto &s : subscribers) s->send(n.to_json());
}

Proposed fixes:
- Add `deliveredVia` metadata to notifications (e.g., ws/poll) or sequence IDs
- On push, mark notification as `deliveredAt` so polls filter delivered items by client id
- Provide idempotency token in responses so front-end can dedupe reliably

Please add fields to the notification store, update APIs, and include migration script for existing notifications.

Long: UI shows 0 instead of '—' for null numeric fields returned by C++ service. Example response:

{ "balance": 0 }

But backend should encode missing vs zero. Code currently serializes an optional<double> incorrectly:

if (balance.has_value()) j["balance"] = *balance; else j["balance"] = 0;

Request:
- Change serialization to omit nulls or set to null explicitly: j["balance"] = balance ? *balance : nullptr;
- Update API docs and front-end to treat absence/null as unavailable and 0 as actual zero.
- Add tests around JSON serialization of optional values (using nlohmann::json).

Long: Performance and UX issue for login flow: on slow auth DB, users stare at 'Logging in...' spinner for many seconds with no feedback. We should implement progressive status updates and a polling token for long-running authentication checks.

Current Auth handler (C++):

Response Auth::login(const Request &req) {
    auto creds = parse(req.body);
    auto user = db.findUser(creds);
    if (!user) return make_error(401);
    auto session = sessionManager.create(user);
    return make_success({{"sessionId", session.id}});
}

Problem: db.findUser can take > 3s and block. UX proposal:
- If db query > 300ms, return 202 with a `statusToken` and endpoint /auth/status/:token that frontend polls for completion
- Provide event stream for notifications
- Log timing metrics and expose `authLatencyMs` in debug endpoint

Please refactor to perform async DB lookup using a thread pool and implement the 202-path. Add unit tests to assert 202 returned when DB op is slow using injection/mocking.

Long: The microservice returns HTML with inline script tags that reference analytics endpoints; this makes CSP strict mode fail and blocks inline script execution. We need to generate a non-blocking, CSP-friendly header and allow script-src with nonce. Steps:
- Add generation of per-response nonce in C++ TemplateEngine and inject into layout header: <meta csp-nonce="...">
- Update templates to use <script nonce="..."> instead of inline JS.

Example C++ changes:

std::string TemplateEngine::render(const Template &t, Context &c) {
    auto nonce = generateNonce();
    res.headers["Content-Security-Policy"] = "script-src 'nonce-" + nonce + "' 'self'";
    c.set("nonce", nonce);
    return mustache::render(t.source, c);
}

Also provide an endpoint /csp/report to collect CSP violations for debugging and add automated tests to assert CSP header present and nonce injected.

Long: Visual regression after recent refactor: table rows don't keep zebra striping when list updates. The REST endpoint returns items with `rowClass` missing. Old contract included `rowType` (even/odd) but was removed during refactor.

Code snippet from ListController.cpp:

json ListController::list(const Request& req) {
    auto items = repo.fetch();
    json out = json::array();
    for (auto &it : items) {
        out.push_back({{"id", it.id}, {"label", it.label}});
    }
    return { {"items", out} };
}

UX needs:
- Add `rowType`/`rowClass` back based on stable sorting
- Ensure the API uses deterministic ordering (created_at DESC, id ASC) so row parity remains stable across pages
- Add unit tests verifying rowClass alternation for sample dataset

Please implement the parity calculation server-side (or provide stable-index) and update docs.

Long: Error handling for image CDN links is inconsistent and causes broken thumbnails in UI. Example error returned:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
{ "thumbnailUrl": "https://cdn/.../thumb.png", "thumbStatus": 502 }

Frontend expects 404 or a missing field to show placeholder. Fixes requested:
- Ensure statuses are reflected via HTTP codes (e.g., 502 -> 502) not embedded fields.
- For degraded thumbnails, return fallbackUrl in response and set `Cache-Control: no-store` so placeholder not cached incorrectly.

Snippet of C++ code handling CDN probe:

int status = probeCdn(url);
json j;
j["thumbnailUrl"] = url;
j["thumbStatus"] = status;

Change to:
if (status != 200) {
    res.set_status(502);
    j["fallbackUrl"] = fallback;
} else {
    res.set_status(200);
    j["thumbnailUrl"] = url;
}

Include integration tests that simulate 502 from CDN and assert client-friendly shape.

Long: UX request — make the microservice provide screenshot thumbnails of user-generated pages to show previews in list. Requirements:
- Add endpoint POST /api/v1/preview that takes { url } and returns { previewId, status, thumbnailUrl }
- Use headless Chromium microservice or a worker to render and capture; the C++ service should enqueue render requests and return 202 with previewId
- Provide GET /api/v1/preview/:id to poll status

Example enqueue code (C++ using producer-consumer queue):

void PreviewService::enqueue(const std::string &url) {
    auto id = uuid::generate();
    queue.push({id, url});
    db.save({id, url, "queued"});
}

Worker will pick up item, call external renderer, store thumbnails in CDN, update DB, and set status to "ready".

Please implement queue, status polling endpoints, retries on render failure, rate limit per user, and TTL for old previews. Include sample integration tests that mock the renderer returning success and failures.

Long: Request to add feedback telemetry for UI changes. UX team wants structured telemetry when users interact with new modal components. Requirements:
- Add POST /api/v1/ui/telemetry to accept small payloads { userId, eventType, componentId, metadata }
- Ensure endpoint is lightweight, rate-limited, authenticates via existing session tokens, and stores data in append-only store (fast path)

C++ handler sketch:

Response Telemetry::post(const Request &req) {
    if (!auth.verify(req.headers["Authorization"])) return make_error(401);
    auto j = nlohmann::json::parse(req.body);
    telemetryQueue.push(j.dump()); // non-blocking
    return make_success({{"accepted", true}});
}

Please implement server-side batching to write telemetry to DB or Kafka, ensure privacy filters to drop PII before enqueue, support opt-out pref via user profile, and add tests verifying rate limits and opt-out respect.

Long: The microservice should support A/B testing for a new UI layout. Need endpoints to: allocate variant, return variant metadata (css tokens, layout flags), and expose assignment analytics.

Requirements:
- Endpoint GET /api/v1/ab/assign?userId=xxx&experiment=home_layout returns { variant: "A" | "B", variantId }
- Assignment must be deterministic given userId and experiment key, with optional override for QA
- Return `variantTokens` object with small CSS variable overrides

Example deterministic assignment helper (C++):

std::string assignVariant(const std::string &userId, const std::string &experiment) {
    std::seed_seq seed(userId.begin(), userId.end());
    std::mt19937 gen(seed);
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> d(0,1);
    return d(gen) == 0 ? "A" : "B";
}

Also add admin endpoints to list experiment assignments and a bulk export for analytics. Ensure privacy by hashing userId and storing only salted hashes for export. Add unit tests to assert consistent assignment and override behavior.

Long: Visual jitter during live updates — list endpoint returns items without a stable key which causes the virtual DOM to re-create elements. Fix by returning `stableKey` for each item derived from a canonical id and version.

Repro: React list uses index as key; backend didn't provide stableKey. Backend change:
- Add `stableKey` = sha1(id + ':' + to_string(version)) in response

C++ code example using OpenSSL SHA1:

std::string stableKey(const std::string &id, int version) {
    std::string input = id + ":" + std::to_string(version);
    unsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    SHA1(reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>(input.c_str()), input.size(), hash);
    return hexEncode(hash, SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH);
}

Please add stableKey to all list endpoints, update docs, and provide migration guidance for the frontend to prefer stableKey as React key to avoid re-mounts.

Long: Intermittent 'Network request failed' on fetch with FormData uploads. Repro steps:
1) Select photo from gallery
2) Tap upload
3) On slow networks, upload fails with 0-length request and error

Client upload code:

async function uploadPhoto(uri) {
  const form = new FormData();
  form.append('file', { uri, name: 'photo.jpg', type: 'image/jpeg' });
  const res = await fetch(API_UPLOAD, { method: 'POST', body: form });
  return res.json();
}

Error log:
TypeError: Network request failed
 at node_modules/react-native/Libraries/Network/fetch.js:…

On Android this seems to be caused by File URI not having content:// scheme for large files. Please normalize URI with RNFetchBlob.wrap or use blob conversion before fetch, and add retry logic for ENOTFOUND/ECONNRESET. Also include progress events.

Long: Upload endpoint returns 413 Request Entity Too Large for certain videos. Client code uses fetch with FormData and no compression. Repro: upload 60s 1080p MP4 on Pixel 4. Server Nginx sets client_max_body_size 50M. Either need client compression or chunked upload. Current client chunk code:

const uploadChunks = async (fileUri) => {
  const size = await getFileSize(fileUri);
  const chunkSize = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5MB
  for (let offset = 0; offset < size; offset += chunkSize) {
    const chunk = await readFileChunk(fileUri, offset, chunkSize);
    await fetch(`${API}/upload/chunk`, { method: 'POST', body: chunk });
  }
}

However readFileChunk uses react-native-fs read method that returns base64 string, and we don't set proper headers or boundaries. Please implement multipart chunk upload with Content-Range headers, retry on 5xx, and a server-side resume API. Also show UX with progress and cancel button.

Long: Repro steps for broken OAuth refresh token flow with code and stack trace.

Symptoms: After token expiry, app attempts to refresh token but fails and throws error, causing user to be logged out.

client/auth.js:

export async function refreshToken() {
  const refresh = await AsyncStorage.getItem('refreshToken');
  const resp = await fetch('/auth/refresh', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ refresh }) });
  const json = await resp.json();
  await AsyncStorage.setItem('accessToken', json.access);
  return json;
}

Error:
TypeError: Network request failed
 at fetch (fetch.js:...) 
 at refreshToken (auth.js:12)

Investigation: backend requires 'Content-Type: application/json' and returns 400 when missing; also if refresh token expired returns 401 with JSON {code: 'refresh_expired'}. Fix client to set headers, handle 400/401 gracefully (prompt re-login), and centralize token refresh logic in an axios/fetch wrapper with single in-flight refresh promise to deduplicate concurrent refresh calls.

Long: Slow query causing profile screen hang on older devices. Code snippet and logs:

async function loadProfile(userId) {
  const res = await fetch(`${API}/users/${userId}/full`);
  const data = await res.json();
  setProfile(data);
}

Server returns huge payload including follower lists and images. On device logcat we see GC spikes and JS thread stalls. Proposed fix: create a lighter endpoint /users/:id/summary for small screens, and lazy-load heavy sections (followers, galleries) with placeholders. Also implement abortable fetch: const controller = new AbortController(); pass controller.signal to fetch and abort if navigation away.

Please refactor client to use summary endpoint and load heavy sections after initial render.

Long: Repro steps and crash when tapping attachment in chat. Error log and component code below.

Stack trace (Sentry):
TypeError: Cannot read property 'uri' of undefined
 at ChatMessage.renderAttachment (ChatMessage.js:78)
 at ChatMessage.render (ChatMessage.js:45)

ChatMessage.js:

renderAttachment() {
  const { attachment } = this.props.message;
  return (
    <TouchableOpacity onPress={() => openAttachment(attachment.uri)}>
      <Image source={{ uri: attachment.uri }} style={{ width: 120, height: 90 }} />
    </TouchableOpacity>
  );
}

Repro: Send a message with no attachment (server sometimes returns message.attachment = null). The component assumes object. Fix by adding guard: if (!attachment || !attachment.uri) return null; and add TypeScript types or PropTypes. Also add unit tests that render ChatMessage with null attachment.

Long: Intermittent 'setState on unmounted component' warnings in FeedList after rapid navigation. Repro: open feed, scroll, quickly back, then re-enter. Warning stack:

Warning: Can't perform a React state update on an unmounted component.
 at FeedList._fetchMore (FeedList.js:98)
 at asyncPromise.then (useEffect.js:23)

FeedList snippet:

componentDidMount() { this._mounted = true; this.fetchInitial(); }
componentWillUnmount() { this._mounted = false; }

async fetchInitial() {
  const data = await api.fetchFeed();
  if (this._mounted) this.setState({ items: data });
}

Problem: some code paths don't check _mounted when awaiting multiple calls or when using AbortController. Please add cancellation tokens / AbortController to fetch, check mounted before every setState, and migrate to functional component with useEffect cleanup returning abort handler.

Long: Android back button exits app unexpectedly on modal screens. Repro: Open settings modal (StackNavigator modal presentation), press hardware back — app exits instead of closing modal. Logcat shows 'BackHandler: invokeDefaultBackPress'. Current code uses BackHandler.addEventListener('hardwareBackPress', onBack) in multiple places without consistent removal. Implementation:

useEffect(() => {
  const onBack = () => { navigation.goBack(); return true; };
  BackHandler.addEventListener('hardwareBackPress', onBack);
  return () => BackHandler.removeEventListener('hardwareBackPress', onBack);
}, []);

However nested listeners conflict. Fix by using BackHandler.addEventListener in top-level navigator and letting navigation handle back actions, or ensure listeners return true only when modal is open; use BackHandler.remove() with the same function reference. Also test Android hardware button behavior across screens.

Long: Crash while parsing push notification payload leading to 'TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object' when reading notification.data.customFields. Example payloads vary between platforms. Error stack:

TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object
 at formatNotification (notifications.js:34)
 at onNotificationReceived (App.js:102)

formatNotification code:

function formatNotification(payload) {
  const fields = Object.keys(payload.data.customFields).map(k => payload.data.customFields[k]);
  return fields.join(', ');
}

Some notifications from third-party provider omit payload.data or data.customFields. Fix by defensive checks: const custom = payload?.data?.customFields || {}; also add schema validation and fallback UI. Add unit tests for notification processing with varied payload shapes.

Long: Performance regression after adding Redux middleware: app startup time increased and splash screen lingers. Trace from profiling shows middleware serializes entire store on each action for debugging:

const debugMiddleware = store => next => action => {
  console.log('action', action, 'state', JSON.stringify(store.getState()));
  return next(action);
}

This JSON.stringify on large state is expensive. Proposed fixes: limit logging to specific actions, debounce logs, or use flatted/circular-safe serializer and run it only in development with __DEV__. Also implement persisted store rehydration optimization (only persist white-listed slices) and move heavy initialization off the critical path (use InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions).

Long: SQLite bindings crash when running complex migrations on Android. Migration script and stack trace below.

Migration:
await db.transaction(tx => {
  tx.executeSql('ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN isSynced INTEGER DEFAULT 0');
  tx.executeSql('UPDATE orders SET isSynced = 1 WHERE synced_at IS NOT NULL');
  tx.executeSql('CREATE INDEX idx_orders_user ON orders(user_id)');
});

Crash log:
FATAL EXCEPTION: SQLiteWorker
java.lang.IllegalStateException: attempt to re-open an already-closed object: SQLiteDatabase
 at android.database.sqlite.SQLiteDatabase.validateOpen(SQLiteDatabase.java:...) 

We suspect multiple concurrent migrations invoked (app startup & background sync). Fix by serializing DB migrations with a singleton migration lock, ensure migrations run only once per version, and add safe checks for table/column existence before running ALTER. Also add try/catch around migration and send error to Sentry.

Long: Repro and fix for missing headers on API requests from mobile app after introducing a new fetch wrapper. Symptoms: some API endpoints return 401 because Authorization header missing. Wrapper code:

export default async function apiFetch(path, opts = {}) {
  const token = await AsyncStorage.getItem('accessToken');
  return fetch(API_BASE + path, { ...opts, headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, ...opts.headers }});
}

Bug: when opts.headers is a Headers instance, spreading it loses entries. Also when opts.headers contains 'Content-Type' set to undefined, it overwrites proper header. Proposed fix: normalize headers with new Headers(), set Authorization via headers.set('Authorization', ...) or build plain object: const headers = Object.assign({}, opts.headers || {}, { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }). Add tests for header merging and verify on iOS/Android.

Long: Repro steps and logs for push token registration failure with Expo on Android 12. Symptoms: deviceToken null and server reports 400 when saving token. Relevant code:

const register = async () => {
  const token = await Notifications.getExpoPushTokenAsync();
  await fetch(`${API}/devices`, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ token: token.data }) });
}

Error in console: UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning: TypeError: Cannot read property 'data' of undefined

getExpoPushTokenAsync returns { data } normally, but on certain Android devices it returns { } or throws. Fix by checking returned object, fallback to Notifications.getDevicePushTokenAsync where applicable, and add retry with exponential backoff. Also ensure server validates payload and returns meaningful error code. Document supported flows for FCM on Android 12.

Long: Users report corrupted Base64 images after converting file using RNFetchBlob.readFile(..., 'base64') and uploading. Repro and code:

const base64 = await RNFetchBlob.fs.readFile(uri.replace('file://', ''), 'base64');
await fetch(API_UPLOAD, { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/octet-stream' }, body: base64 });

Server rejects with 'invalid image data'. Investigation shows fetch with base64 string sends text without proper binary decoding. Fix by converting base64 to Blob via atob/Uint8Array and sending Blob, or use RNFetchBlob.fetch to send binary with proper content-type and encoding: RNFetchBlob.fetch('POST', API_UPLOAD, { 'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data' }, [{ name: 'file', filename: 'photo.jpg', data: base64 }]). Also add size checks and fallback for very large images.

Long: Repro and fix for gradient background rendering black on Android when using LinearGradient with Animated value. Symptoms: gradient shows for 1 frame then turns black when animating opacity. Code:

const opacity = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;
Animated.timing(opacity, { toValue: 1, duration: 300 }).start();
return (
  <Animated.View style={{ opacity }}>
    <LinearGradient colors={["#fff", "#000"]} style={{ flex: 1 }} />
  </Animated.View>
);

Issue: Android hardware acceleration plus compositing of gradient inside Animated.View causes rendering bug. Workarounds: animate transform or use Animated.createAnimatedComponent(LinearGradient) to animate gradient directly, or avoid animating parent opacity and instead animate gradient stop positions. Implement an AnimatedLinearGradient wrapper and test across Android versions.

Long: Repro and solution for 'Unhandled Promise Rejection' in offline sync module when SQLite insert fails. Error and code:

UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning: Error: UNIQUE constraint failed: items.remoteId
 at Database._executeSql (sqlite.js:123)

sync.js:

async function syncItem(item) {
  const res = await fetch(`${API}/items`, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(item) });
  const json = await res.json();
