Implement a VM-based plugin loader using Node's vm.Module or vm.Script and a restricted context exposing only limited APIs. Provide example code and warn about limitations. Example:

const vm = require('vm');
function loadPlugin(code, opts){
  const context = { console, require: limitedRequire(opts), Buffer: undefined };
  return vm.runInNewContext(code, context);
}

LONG: Crash when config contains circular references parsed from YAML anchors. Example .cli-config.yaml:
base: &base
  timeout: 3000
service:
  <<: *base
  retries: 3

Error on load:
RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
    at Object.assign (<anonymous>)
    at deepMerge (/usr/local/lib/cli/lib/config.js:78:18)

We need to detect YAML anchors that create cycles and gracefully handle them (js-yaml usually resolves anchors, but our deep merge is recursing). Fix: keep a WeakSet of visited nodes during deep merge and stop recursion when encountering previously visited objects. Code suggestion:

function deepMerge(target, source, seen = new WeakSet()){
  if (seen.has(source)) return target;
  seen.add(source);
  // merge logic...
}

LONG: The CLI should support per-command config via package.json scripts. We saw users set config in package.json under 'cliConfig' but our loader ignores it. Example package.json snippet:
{
  "name": "app",
  "cliConfig": { "api": { "baseUrl": "https://dev.api" } }
}

Request: enhance the loader to read package.json's cliConfig as lowest-precedence config if present. Implementation detail: search upward from process.cwd() to find package.json and parse cliConfig. Also ensure package.json parsing errors are handled gracefully with clear diagnostics.

LONG: Feature request: allow dynamic config reloading for long-running CLI modes (daemon/watch). Problem: config changes on disk aren't picked up. Proposed behavior: watch detected config files and emit 'config:changed' event with previous and next configs. Provide code sketch using chokidar:

const chokidar = require('chokidar');
function watchConfig(file){
  const watcher = chokidar.watch(file);
  watcher.on('change', async () => {
    const newCfg = await loadConfig(file);
    emitter.emit('config:changed', { old: currentCfg, new: newCfg });
    currentCfg = newCfg;
  });
}

Include opt-in config.watch: true to avoid unexpected reloads in CI.

LONG: Secure defaults are missing: config.telemetry defaults to true but enterprise policy requires explicit opt-in. Change default to false and add migration warning when upgrading. Provide migration script that sets telemetry.enabled=false for existing users unless they opted in. Also update onboarding to explain privacy settings and where telemetry endpoints are configured.

Suggested migration script example:

const cfg = loadConfig();
if (cfg.telemetry === undefined) cfg.telemetry = { enabled: false };
saveConfig(cfg);

LONG: Inconsistent path resolution for 'workDir' option. Repro:
- .cli-config.json sets workDir: './build'
- Running CLI from project root works, but running from a subdirectory uses different base and fails to find files

Trace:
Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/home/user/project/src/build/index.js'
    at Object.openSync (fs.js:xxx)

Fix: resolve relative paths relative to the config file's directory (or a configured baseDir). Example helper:

function resolvePath(cfgPath, p){
  const base = cfgPath ? path.dirname(cfgPath) : process.cwd();
  return path.resolve(base, p);
}

Also add option config.baseDir to override resolution root.

LONG: Error when merging config arrays of objects with missing unique keys. Example plugin list:
[
  { "name": "a", "config": {} },
  { "config": {} }
]

Merge logic expects 'name' keys and throws:
TypeError: Cannot read property 'name' of undefined
    at Map.set (<anonymous>)

Request: validate array items to ensure required keys exist (name, id) and provide helpful error messages with index. Also support fallback dedup strategy by serializing whole object when 'name' is missing. Example validation snippet:

cfg.plugins.forEach((p,i)=>{ if (!p.name) throw new Error(`plugins[${i}] missing 'name'`); });

LONG: Problem: CLI's plugin discovery uses require() on arbitrary package names from config.plugins which opens RCE possibility in shared config scenarios. Need to add safe-mode config: config.plugins.safeLoad = true which will sandbox plugin code in a child process and communicate over IPC. Provide IPC contract and fallback to direct require when safeLoad=false. Include sample child loader code:

// child-loader.js
process.on('message', ({modulePath}) => {
  try { const mod = require(modulePath); process.send({ ok: true }); } catch (e) { process.send({ ok: false, error: e.message }); }
});

Parent spawns child for each plugin when safeLoad enabled.

LONG: CLI currently writes config files using fs.writeFileSync which can corrupt files if interrupted. Need to write atomically: write to temp file then rename. Provide code example using fs.promises:

async function writeAtomic(filePath, data){
  const tmp = filePath + '.' + process.pid + '.tmp';
  await fs.promises.writeFile(tmp, data, { mode: 0o600 });
  await fs.promises.rename(tmp, filePath);
}

Also ensure file permissions for secrets are set to 600 on Unix and use platform-appropriate APIs on Windows.

LONG: Add support for config templates with variables. Example: team wants to manage templates with placeholders and render them per-repo. Use case:

template:
  name: 'node-service'
  content: |
    apiBase: {{API_BASE}}

Need to implement a simple templating engine (mustache-like) and a cli command 'config render --vars API_BASE=https://api.example.com' that outputs filled config. Include security note about not rendering secrets into checked-in files. Example JS snippet using mustache:

const Mustache = require('mustache');
const result = Mustache.render(template.content, vars);

LONG: Audit and fix race condition when multiple concurrent CLI processes try to initialize the default config directory. Repro: run 'cli init' in parallel in CI; one process may create directory while another reads/writes and race causes ENOENT or partial files. Proposed fix: implement file-based lock using proper atomic operations (fs.open with O_EXCL or flock). Example with proper locking:

const lockfile = require('proper-lockfile');
await lockfile.lock(dir, { retries: { retries: 5, minTimeout: 50 } });
// perform init
await lockfile.unlock(dir);

LONG: Need to support backwards compatibility with old boolean-negated CLI flags being stored in config as 'no-telemetry: true'. Scenario: older config files used keys prefixed with 'no-' to invert flags. Newer code checks telemetry.enabled. Implement a migration wrapper that maps known 'no-' prefixed keys into their positive equivalents and logs migrations. Example mapping:

const negations = { 'no-telemetry': ['telemetry', 'enabled'] };
function migrateNegations(cfg){
  Object.keys(cfg).forEach(k => { if (k.startsWith('no-')) { const val = cfg[k]; const target = k.slice(3); cfg[target] = { enabled: !val }; delete cfg[k]; } });
}

Long: Wrong TypeScript typings for error handler middleware causing developer confusion.

Current signature in types.d.ts:

export type ErrorMiddleware = (err: any, next: () => void) => void;

Problems: err:any loses typing; next should accept an optional transformed error to continue chain. Request: change signature to:

export type ErrorMiddleware = (err: ISDKError, next: (err?: ISDKError) => Promise<void> | void) => Promise<void> | void;

Example transformation middleware:

const redactPII: ErrorMiddleware = async (err, next) => {
  if (err.payload?.email) err.payload.email = '<redacted>';
  await next(err);
};

Add tests for middleware composition and update docs. Also export ISDKError interface from main package so consumers can implement middleware with proper typing.

Long: We're getting 'TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object' inside error merging utility.

Stack trace from Node:
TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object
    at Object.assign (<anonymous>)
    at mergeErrorContext (/src/errors/merge.ts:22:12)
    at Client.request (/src/client.ts:145:20)
    at processTicksAndRejections (internal/process/task_queues.js:93:5)

Relevant code:

// merge.ts
export function mergeErrorContext(base: ISDKError, ctx?: Record<string, any>) {
  return Object.assign({}, base, ctx);
}

Bug: ctx is sometimes null from telemetry. Fix by defaulting ctx = {} and use deepMerge to avoid overwriting nested objects. Add tests where ctx is null and where ctx contains nested objects to ensure deterministic merging.

Long: Inconsistent stack traces after wrapping errors with new classes. Example code:

class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(message, code) {
    super(message);
    this.code = code;
  }
}

async function fetchUser() {
  try {
    await sdk.request('/user');
  } catch (e) {
    throw new ApiError('Fetch failed', 'FETCH_FAILED');
  }
}

Observed stack (missing original stack):
ApiError: Fetch failed
    at fetchUser (/dist/index.js:30:15)

Desired: preserve original stack as cause or append stack traces.

Node supports error.cause. Update our wrappers to: new ApiError('...', { cause: originalError }) and for older targets, set apiError.stack = `${apiError.stack}\nCaused by: ${originalError.stack}`. Add tests verifying stack contains both messages. Also update TS types to include optional cause: Error in custom error constructors.

Long: File upload handler returns inconsistent error shapes for server validation failures. Repro and code below.

Client usage:
await client.uploadFile(fileStream, { metadata: { title: '' } });

Server returns 400 with body:
{
  "errors": [{ "field": "title", "message": "Title is required" }]
}

Current thrown error (TS):
Error: Bad Request
  name: 'Error',
  status: 400,
  body: '{"errors":[...] }'

Desired: throw ValidationError with typed fieldErrors: Array<{ field: string; message: string }>. Update uploadFile signature to reject with ValidationError | SDKApiError and include example code for consumers:

try {
  await client.uploadFile(...)
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) console.log(err.fieldErrors);
}

Add tests mocking 400 and asserting the thrown error is ValidationError with parsed fieldErrors typed as expected.

Long: Incorrect typings for error factory that create codes and messages. Example and failing compile:

// errors.ts
export const createError = (code: string, message: string) => ({ code, message, toString() { return message } });

// usage somewhere
const err = createError('NOT_FOUND', 'Not found');
if (err.code === 'NOT_FOUND') { /* ok */ }

TypeScript complain: Property 'code' does not exist on type 'string | ...'. Investigation shows createError returned a string in some overloads after optimization.

Fix: strictly type factory as returning ISDKError and export interface ISDKError { code: string; message: string; name?: string; status?: number }.
Add tests ensuring TS compile for consumer code like:
const err: ISDKError = createError('A', 'b');

Also check bundler side-effects causing tree-shaking to replace object with primitive in prod builds; adjust build config so error factory isn't inlined incorrectly.

Long: Race condition in token refresh: multiple parallel requests see 401 and all trigger refresh, producing multiple refresh calls and invalidating tokens. Repro steps and code snippet:

// client.ts
if (response.status === 401) {
  await auth.refreshToken();
  return this.request(original);
}

When N requests fire concurrently, they all call refreshToken. Desired behavior: single-flight token refresh (dedupe). Patch: implement refreshLock Promise cached on in-flight refresh and await it in other callers. Example implementation:

let refreshPromise: Promise<void> | null = null;
async function ensureToken() {
  if (!refreshPromise) refreshPromise = auth.refreshToken().finally(() => refreshPromise = null);
  await refreshPromise;
}

Add tests simulating parallel 401s with msw and assert auth.refreshToken called once and other requests wait for it.

Long: The SDK's error mapper discards nested error details. Example server error:

HTTP 500
{
  "error": {
    "code": "DB_ERROR",
    "message": "unique constraint",
    "meta": { "constraint": "users_email_key", "query": "INSERT..." }
  }
}

Current thrown error only has message 'unique constraint'. We need to attach meta as error.meta and map DB error codes to user-friendly codes like ConflictError. Proposed change: update parseErrorBody to look for common shapes (error, errors, message) and merge nested details into ISDKError.meta. Update types to reflect meta?: Record<string, any>. Provide code snippet for parseErrorBody.

Also add mapping table for known DB messages to SDK error types and tests mocking 500 with DB payload verifying thrown error.meta.constraint === 'users_email_key'.

Long: Using the SDK in SvelteKit causes uncaught exceptions when errors are serialized to JSON during server-side rendering. Error message and stack:

TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON
    at JSON.stringify (<anonymous>)
    at Object.serializeError (/dist/errors.js:40:22)

The SSR runtime serializes props by calling toJSON on returned objects. Our SDKError.toJSON returns the entire request object causing circular references. Fix: implement toJSON that returns only safe fields: { name, message, code, status, truncatedPayload } and use a safe stringify in the serializer function. Add unit test that simulates SSR serialization and asserts no exception thrown. Provide code example for toJSON:

class SDKError extends Error {
  toJSON() {
    return { name: this.name, message: this.message, code: this.code, status: this.status };
  }
}

Also document how consumers can opt into including request info during debug builds only.

Long: When bundling SDK for browser with esbuild, custom Error subclasses lose prototype chain leading instanceof checks to fail in consumer apps.

Observation: After bundling, `err instanceof SDKError` is false.

Minimal reproduction:

// src/errors.ts
export class SDKError extends Error {}

// dist bundle (esbuild) transforms classes in a way that breaks instanceof across module boundaries.

Fix: ensure tsconfig and bundler target preserve class semantics (use native class output) and add __proto__ fix for older targets:

if (typeof Object.setPrototypeOf === 'function') Object.setPrototypeOf(SDKError.prototype, Error.prototype);

Also recommend exporting an isSDKError helper:

export function isSDKError(e: any): e is SDKError { return e && e.name === 'SDKError'; }

Add integration test where app bundles SDK and asserts instanceof and helper both work.

Long: Failure to retry idempotent requests due to misclassification of methods. Details and suggested patch.

Issue: SDK only retries GET but POST to /items/retryable is idempotent in our API (safe to retry). Users want ability to mark a request as idempotent so client retries on network errors.

Current code:
if (method === 'GET' || method === 'HEAD') retry = true;

Request API needs: client.request({ method, path, idempotent?: boolean }) and retry logic should include idempotent flag. Add TypeScript interface change and update retry manager. Example:

interface RequestOptions { method: HttpMethod; idempotent?: boolean; }

const shouldRetry = options.idempotent || method === 'GET' || method === 'HEAD';

Add tests where POST marked idempotent triggers retry and ensure side effects are safe; update docs explaining responsibility of users to only mark idempotent when safe.

Long: Error handling in streaming responses (ReadableStream) is poor: when the stream errors we swallow error and return partial data. Repro steps and code:

const resp = await client.stream('/big');
try {
  for await (const chunk of resp.body) { process(chunk); }
} catch (e) {
  // SDK catches here and returns { ok: false } silently
}

Log shows: 'Stream ended prematurely' but no error thrown to user. Fix: rethrow stream errors to consumer and attach error.streamPosition and partialBytes. Also add abort handling so consumers can abort stream via AbortController and receive SDKAbortError with aborted flag.

Proposed change: in stream wrapper add

resp.body.on('error', (err) => {
  const sdkErr = new StreamError('Stream failed', { cause: err, partialBytes });
  reject(sdkErr);
});

Add tests using node stream that emits 'error' after some chunks and assert the consumer catches StreamError with partialBytes > 0.

Long: Client-side SDK shows 'TypeError: Failed to fetch' with no status when CORS preflight fails. Repro and patch:

Repro:
1) Browser calls client.get('/api/data')
2) Server missing Access-Control-Allow-Origin -> request fails
3) SDK throws generic TypeError: Failed to fetch

We should throw SDKNetworkError with code 'CORS_ERROR' and helpful diagnostics (requestedUrl, origin, suggestedFix). Since the browser network stack doesn't expose response, attempt a best-effort detection: if navigator.onLine === false -> OFFLINE, else if request fails immediately and mode === 'cors' -> CORS_ERROR. Include example change in fetch wrapper:

try { await fetch(...) } catch (e) {
  if (typeof e === 'object' && e.message === 'Failed to fetch' && init.mode === 'cors') {
    throw new SDKNetworkError('CORS_ERROR', { url, origin: location.origin });
  }
  throw e;
}

Add docs showing what to check server-side to fix CORS and tests using Puppeteer to simulate missing CORS headers.

Long: Error chaining and preservation across async boundaries is inconsistent in TypeScript generics. Example failing scenario and types:

// types.ts
export class TypedError<T> extends Error { constructor(public payload: T, message: string) { super(message); } }

async function foo<T>() {
  try {
    await bar<T>();
  } catch (e) {
    throw new TypedError<T>(e.payload, 'wrapped');
  }
}

TypeScript complaint: property 'payload' does not exist on type 'unknown'. We need stricter typing for caught errors: use 'catch (e: unknown)' and then narrow: if (isTypedError(e)) {...}

Request: update SDK examples to use safe catch typing and provide helper type guards isSDKError<T>(e): e is TypedError<T>. Also change library internals to avoid assuming Error<any> shape; always check error && typeof error === 'object'. Provide code snippets for type guard and improved catch usage, and update lint rules to no-unsafe-member-access in catch.

Long: When SDK is used in a lambda behind API Gateway, errors thrown by the SDK are being stringified as '[object Object]' in CloudWatch due to console.log on an Error object with custom properties. Example log lines:

ERROR: [object Object]

We should update logger to use JSON.stringify safe serializer for objects and include error.stack. Also implement Error.prototype.toLog which returns { name, message, stack, ...meta } and make logger detect Error instances and call toLog. Provide code:

function normalizeForLog(err: any) {
  if (err instanceof Error) return ({ name: err.name, message: err.message, stack: err.stack, ...((err as any).meta || {}) });
  return err;
}

Add tests that simulate passing SDKError with meta to logger and assert console output is JSON string with stack included.

Long: We're seeing high memory usage because some handlers clone large request DTOs unnecessarily. Please audit handlers in src/handlers/*.rs and change signatures to take references where possible. Example:

Before:
async fn create_post(pool: PgPool, body: CreatePost) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
    let user_id = body.author_id.clone();
    // more clones
}

After:
async fn create_post(pool: &PgPool, body: &CreatePost) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
    let user_id = &body.author_id;
}

Notes: The routes are wired with actix-web's web::Json<CreatePost>. You may need to change extractors to web::Json<CreatePost> and then pass &body into service layer. Update signatures across service layer (PostService) and update tests. Also run cargo bloat to verify improvements.

Long: Intermittent serialization errors observed in logs:

ERROR api::handlers::events: failed to serialize response: Serde(Error("duplicate field `type`", line: 1, column: 23))

This happens when merging event payload maps and serializing with serde_json::to_string. The merging helper currently does manual insert that may collide. Search for merge_maps or combine_payload functions, and replace with an explicit struct model for events instead of dynamic HashMap. Example offending code:

let mut payload = serde_json::Map::new();
payload.insert("type".to_string(), Value::String(event_type));
for (k, v) in extra_fields { payload.insert(k.clone(), v.clone()); } // duplicates possible
let body = Value::Object(payload);

Please refactor to a strongly-typed EventPayload struct with derive(Serialize) and keep duplicates prevented at construction time. Add a failing unit test that demonstrates duplicate key causing error and fix it.

Long: CI failing with clippy lints: 'manual_memcpy' and 'useless_vec' across src/utils/*.rs. The problematic snippet:

let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(data.len());
for &b in data.iter() { buf.push(b); }

Suggested cleanup: replace with let buf = data.to_vec(); or buf.extend_from_slice(data). Also replace mem::zeroed usage with MaybeUninit where appropriate. Please run cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features and fix all warnings. Also update rust-toolchain if needed.

Provide commit message and ensure all tests pass locally before pushing.

Long: Error observed when posting multipart form with file and JSON fields simultaneously. Error:

actix_web::error::JsonPayloadError: Content type is not `application/json`

Handler signature currently expects web::Json<CreateThing> while we need multipart extraction. Example code:

async fn upload_create(mut payload: Multipart, json: web::Json<CreateThing>) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
  // unreachable because content-type is multipart/form-data
}

Please convert handler to parse multipart manually and parse the JSON part using serde_json::from_slice. Provide code snippet fix:

let mut json_part: Option<CreateThing> = None;
while let Some(item) = payload.next().await {
    let mut field = item?;
    if field.name() == "metadata" {
        let mut bytes = web::BytesMut::new();
        while let Some(chunk) = field.next().await { bytes.extend_from_slice(&chunk?); }
        json_part = Some(serde_json::from_slice(&bytes)?);
    }
}

Also add integration test to post both parts and assert response 201.

Long: Several endpoints do manual timestamp conversion using UNIX epoch math leading to timezone bugs. Example code in src/models/mod.rs:

fn iso_timestamp(ts: i64) -> String {
    let naive = NaiveDateTime::from_timestamp(ts, 0);
    naive.format("%+\").to_string()
}

This returns local time on some hosts. Replace with chrono::DateTime<Utc>::from_utc and use to_rfc3339_opts(SecondsFormat::Secs, true). Update all places that format timestamps for JSON responses. Add unit tests verifying formatting for known epoch values and ensure serialization derives use serde(with = "chrono::serde::ts_seconds") where appropriate.

Long: Race condition in cached lookup: using lazy_static HashMap with Mutex but we forgot to handle poisoning. Crash trace:

thread 'tokio-runtime-worker' panicked at 'called Result::unwrap() on an Err value', src/cache/mod.rs:53:18

Current code:

lazy_static! {
    static ref CACHE: Mutex<HashMap<String, CachedVal>> = Mutex::new(HashMap::new());
}

fn get_cached(k: &str) -> Option<CachedVal> {
    let lock = CACHE.lock().unwrap(); // panics on poisoning
    lock.get(k).cloned()
}

Fix: replace unwrap() with map_err to log and recreate state or switch to parking_lot::Mutex which doesn't poison. Consider using dashmap for concurrent reads and writes. Add tests that simulate panic inside a lock and verify service doesn't crash.

Long: API returns inconsistent error shapes: some handlers return { "error": "msg" } and others return { "code": 400, "message": "msg" }. Need to standardize on ErrorResponse { code: u16, message: String, details: Option<Value> } and implement From<ServiceError> for actix_web::Error mapping to HttpResponse. Tasks:
- Define ErrorResponse in crate::api::errors
- Replace manual HttpResponse::BadRequest().json(...) with ApiError conversions
- Update swagger docs and client generator config
- Add unit tests asserting shape of common error cases (validation, auth, db)

Example desired mapping:

impl ResponseError for ApiError {
    fn status_code(&self) -> StatusCode { ... }
    fn error_response(&self) -> HttpResponse {
        HttpResponse::build(self.status_code()).json(ErrorResponse::from(self))
    }
}

Please ensure compatible with existing frontend expectations.

Long: We have a memory leak in websocket handler where each connection spawns a task capturing self without cancellation. Crash excerpt:

TASK WARNING: 10,000 tasks pending; memory usage steadily rising

Code snippet:

async fn ws_handler(req: HttpRequest, stream: Payload, state: Data<AppState>) {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::unbounded_channel();
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        loop {
            let msg = rx.recv().await.unwrap();
            // process
        }
    });
}

Because tx/receivers are not dropped on disconnect, tasks live forever. Fix by using actix-web's ws::WebsocketContext and stream combinators that drop tasks on disconnect; or ensure Oneshot shutdown signal and cancellation via AbortHandle. Add tests to simulate 1000 connects/disconnects to confirm no leak. Also update monitoring metrics for active websocket tasks.

Long: Code cleanup request: many modules use Result<T, Box<dyn std::error::Error>>. Replace with concrete Result<T, ApiError> and derive Debug/Display using thiserror::Error. Example current code in src/service/email.rs:

pub fn send_email(...) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // possible SmtpError, IoError
}

Change to:

#[derive(thiserror::Error, Debug)]
pub enum EmailError {
    #[error("smtp: {0}")]
    Smtp(#[from] lettre::error::Error),
    #[error("io: {0}")]
    Io(#[from] std::io::Error),
}

pub fn send_email(...) -> Result<(), EmailError> { ... }

Then map EmailError into ApiError for handlers. Update Cargo.toml with thiserror dependency if not present. Add tests verifying Error::source and Display output.

Long: Slow response times traced to serde_json building Value trees repeatedly in middleware. Example middleware currently clones entire request JSON to attach tracing fields:

let mut body: Value = serde_json::from_slice(&bytes).unwrap();
body["trace_id"] = json!(trace_id);
let new_bytes = serde_json::to_vec(&body).unwrap();

Replace approach with streaming JSON parser or modify request body only in logs and avoid writing back modified body to pipeline. Instead, attach trace_id in request extensions (req.extensions_mut()) for downstream handlers to use. Update tests for middleware accordingly and remove eager allocation patterns.

Long: The multipart uploader leaks temp files sometimes; using tempfile::NamedTempFile without explicit persist causes files to remain when process is killed. Reproduce steps:

1) Upload large file and kill the server during upload
2) Observe /tmp has many temp files owned by service

Current handler:

let mut tmp = NamedTempFile::new()?;
while let Some(chunk) = field.next().await { tmp.write_all(&chunk?)?; }
// missing cleanup on error

Fix: use tempfile::Builder and ensure files are created in a configured temp dir and register cleanup on shutdown (tokio::spawn async drop guard). Alternatively, stream directly to S3 using multipart upload without writing locally. Add integration test that kills process and ensures no leftover files when restarted.

Long: The API gateway expects X-Request-Id header but our middleware currently generates 'x_request_id' in lowercase. Fix case sensitivity and make header canonicalized. Also ensure tracing span includes request_id and that it's returned in responses. Current middleware snippet:

req.headers_mut().insert("x_request_id", HeaderValue::from_str(&uuid).unwrap());

Change to:

let header_name = HeaderName::from_static("x-request-id");
req.headers_mut().insert(header_name.clone(), HeaderValue::from_str(&uuid)?);
res.headers_mut().insert(header_name, HeaderValue::from_str(&uuid)?);

Add tests to ensure responses include header and update frontend expectations.

Long: Fix database transaction usage: some service functions call .begin() twice or start nested transactions leading to deadlocks. Example code path:

let mut tx = pool.begin().await?;
create_user(&mut tx, ...).await?;
let inner = pool.begin().await?; // wrong: should use &mut tx
inner.execute(...).await?;
tx.commit().await?;

Refactor repository functions to accept &mut Transaction<'_, Postgres> and avoid creating new transactions. Add integration test for concurrent user creation to ensure no deadlocks. Also enable sqlx offline mode and ensure queries match DB schema.

Long: Code cleanup: replace manual JSON parsing with strongly typed request structs everywhere. Example current handler in src/handlers/metrics.rs:

let body: Value = serde_json::from_slice(&bytes)?;
let id = body["id"].as_str().unwrap();

Replace with:

#[derive(Deserialize)] struct MetricIn { id: String, value: f64 }
let body: MetricIn = serde_json::from_slice(&bytes)?;

This reduces panics and makes fields explicit for docs. Update endpoints, update client integration tests, and add fallback error mapping for missing fields returning 400 with validation details.

Long: We need to support remote configuration for enterprise deployments. Desired behavior: on first run, if a file C:\ProgramData\MyApp\config.json exists, use that to pre-populate settings and disable auto-update if specified. Problems observed: per-machine install doesn't read ProgramData on Windows due to permission issues when running as non-admin. Implementation details: installer should write a read-only config to ProgramData with proper ACLs (read for Users). App should check ProgramData path first, then per-user config. Provide code for reading both locations and merging with precedence:

import json
from pathlib import Path

def load_config():
    machine = Path(r'C:\ProgramData\MyApp\config.json')
    user = Path(os.getenv('APPDATA')) / 'MyApp' / 'config.json'
    cfg = {}
    if machine.exists():
        cfg.update(json.load(machine.open('r')))
    if user.exists():
        cfg.update(json.load(user.open('r')))
    return cfg

Also ensure installer sets file ACLs with icacls to grant read to 'Users' and document the schema for enterprise admins.

Long: We're seeing huge log volume because structured maps are logged inline and sometimes contain hundreds of keys (e.g., response headers). Need a configurable max map depth/size and truncation policy. Example log line:

{"level":"info","msg":"response","headers":{"Accept-Encoding":"gzip","User-Agent":"...lots...","Cookie":"...lots..."},"body":"...huge..."}

Desired behavior: if a map has more than 20 keys, replace excess with "_truncated":N and optionally redact values for keys matching regex like "cookie|authorization|set-cookie".

Proposed API:

logger := loglib.NewLogger(loglib.Config{
    MaxMapEntries: 20,
    RedactKeys: []string{"(?i)cookie","(?i)authorization"},
})

Please implement map truncation and redaction at encoder time (not requiring caller to pre-filter). Add unit tests and benchmark for encoding performance with large maps. Ensure behavior is documented.

Long: The async buffer drops logs silently when the outbound HTTP sink returns 429 or 503 rapidly. Here's our production log of drops and a relevant snippet:

time="2025-07-12T10:12:03Z" level=warn msg="sink dropped 450 logs due to full queue" queue_size=1000 sink=remote

HTTP sink client code:

func (s *remoteSink) pushBatch(ctx context.Context, batch []byte) error {
    req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, "POST", s.url, bytes.NewReader(batch))
    req.Header.Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
    resp, err := s.client.Do(req)
    if err != nil { return err }
    if resp.StatusCode >= 500 || resp.StatusCode == 429 {
        return fmt.Errorf("server error: %d", resp.StatusCode)
    }
    return nil
}

Currently, the worker returns error and increments a counter but silently drops items when buffer is full. We need: (1) exponential backoff with jitter for retries on 429/5xx; (2) a bounded retry queue with TTL per batch; (3) metrics for retries and permanent drops; (4) option to block producers instead of dropping (configurable). Please add tests that simulate failing server and verify behavior, and include config knobs like MaxRetries, RetryBaseDelay, RetryMaxDelay, RetryTTL, BackoffJitter.

Long: We need a sample that wires the library into Kubernetes deployments where logs are consumed by Fluentd. Currently logs have a top-level `timestamp` and nested `msg` which Fluentd doesn't parse correctly. Fluentd expects `time` and `log` fields. Request: add a configurable compatibility mode for Kubernetes/Fluentd that maps fields accordingly and supports adding Kubernetes metadata from downward API if provided. Example config:

logger := loglib.NewLogger(loglib.Config{
    KubeCompatibility: true,
    KubePodNameEnv: "MY_POD_NAME",
})

Desired output:
{"time":"2025-07-12T12:00:00Z","log":"user login succeeded","severity":"INFO","kubernetes":{"pod_name":"...","namespace":"..."}}

Please implement the mapping and provide an example Dockerfile and manifest showing how to set the downward API env vars and run a sidecar collector with Fluentd. Add unit tests for the encoder mapping.

Long: When using the library with Google Cloud Logging (Stackdriver), logs are being rejected due to missing severity field mapping and incorrect timestamp format. Here is a recorded error from cloud logging API:

{"error": {"code": 400, "message": "LogEntry validation failed: severity must be one of [DEFAULT, DEBUG, INFO, NOTICE, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL, ALERT, EMERGENCY]"}}

Our logs currently emit "level":"warn" and timestamps like "2025-07-12T10:00:00.123Z" which is OK but severity mismatch causes rejections. Request: add a CloudLoggingSink with proper severity mapping (map warn->WARNING, info->INFO, debug->DEBUG, error->ERROR) and an option to set the resource & labels. Also support the Cloud Logging HTTP v2 API with token authentication. Provide tests mocking the API and an example of configuring credentials using ADC (Application Default Credentials).

Long: Tracebacks are hard to correlate to logs because we currently include goroutine IDs inconsistently. We need: a) a function to capture goroutine ID (best-effort) and attach to log entries as gID, b) ensure that goroutine id is captured only for debug/error levels (configurable), c) avoid high allocation overhead. Here's a suggested implementation snippet that some projects use (risky but works):

func getGID() uint64 {
    b := make([]byte, 64)
    b = b[:runtime.Stack(b, false)]
    // parse like "goroutine 123 ["
    // ... parse logic
}

Problems: runtime.Stack is expensive. Please propose an efficient design: maybe sample goroutine IDs only when reporting a panic/stacktrace; provide an opt-in CaptureGID bool and document tradeoffs. Add benchmarks demonstrating cost and implement tests ensuring no crash. Also provide a safe fallback when parsing fails.

Long: The library's context-based logger loses fields when using go routines and channels: we set ctx = logger.With(ctx, Field("req", id)) and then spawn multiple goroutines that read ctx from a channel; some logs emitted inside goroutines don't include req. Minimal example:

func handler(ctx context.Context) {
    ctx = loglib.WithField(ctx, "req", "123")
    ch := make(chan context.Context, 1)
    ch <- ctx
    go func() {
        ctx := <-ch
        loglib.FromContext(ctx).Info("inside goroutine") // missing req field
    }()
}

I suspect WithField attaches a pointer that gets mutated later. Suggestion: ensure context values are immutable copies, or use logger.WithFields that returns a new logger bound into context with immutable fields. Please fix semantics and add tests demonstrating correct propagation across goroutine boundaries and channel passes.

Long: The log encoding for errors currently uses fmt.Sprintf("%+v", err) which prints wrapped errors nicely, but some errors implement Error() that includes sensitive data. Request: add an ErrFormatter interface support so types can implement SafeError() string to control what gets logged if present; otherwise default to errors.Unwrap chain but redact sensitive tokens via regex. Add examples and tests.

Sample code:

type SafeError interface {
    SafeError() string
}

func encodeError(e error) string {
    if se, ok := e.(SafeError); ok { return se.SafeError() }
    // fallback to unwrap chain but redact
}

Also provide a configuration toggle LogFullErrorStack bool to include stack traces in logs for error levels >= error.

Long: Need better testability for log output ordering. In our tests we rely on logs appearing in FIFO order, but the async writer may reorder due to batching. Request: add an option to preserve ordering by sequence number and an assertion helper AssertLogsOrdered(t, []string) which waits and compares or provide a sync writer for tests. Example test flakiness:

func TestProcess(t *testing.T) {
    logger := loglib.NewTestLogger()
    logger.Info("start")
    go processItem()
    logger.Info("end")
    // currently sometimes "end" appears before some process logs
    loglib.AssertLogsOrdered(t, []string{"start","process","end"})
}

Please implement TestLogger that is deterministic and document tradeoffs.

Long: The library currently ignores context deadlines when flushing async logs on shutdown; our process sometimes waits indefinitely because remote sink hang prevents shutdown. Need to wire ctx.Context deadlines to flush operations: when Shutdown(ctx) is called, the flush should respect ctx.Done() and return error if timeout. Also make the default Shutdown timeout configurable and ensure graceful fallback to synchronous flush for critical sinks like audit. Example change:

func (l *Logger) Shutdown(ctx context.Context) error {
    // current implementation blocks
}

Desired: honor ctx cancellation and return error if flush incomplete. Add tests that simulate hanging sink and ensure Shutdown returns within timeout and resources cleaned.

LONG: String encoding corruption on iOS simulator when loading localized .json files. Repro steps:
1) Build on macOS with Xcode 13.
2) Launch simulator with locale set to Japanese.
3) App displays mojibake for strings containing '–' and '…'.

Console output:
2025-10-12 12:34:56.789 MyApp[12345:67890] Failed to convert string: C3 80 C2 A6

Relevant loader code (LocalizationLoader.cpp):

std::string LocalizationLoader::readFile(const std::string& path) {
    std::ifstream f(path, std::ios::binary);
    std::ostringstream ss;
    ss << f.rdbuf();
    std::string s = ss.str();
    // current code erroneously assumes UTF-16 if BOM present, but BOM detection broken
    if (s.size() >= 2 && (unsigned char)s[0] == 0xFF && (unsigned char)s[1] == 0xFE) {
        // wchar_t conversion
        std::wstring ws(reinterpret_cast<wchar_t*>(...)); // unsafe
        return std::string(ws.begin(), ws.end());
    }
    return s; // returned string may not be valid UTF-8
}

We should standardize on UTF-8, explicitly detect BOMs, avoid reinterpret_cast to wchar_t, and use iconv or ICU conversion to normalize to UTF-8. Add unit tests for files with UTF-8, UTF-16LE/BE BOMs and no BOM.

LONG: Search feature returns wrong collation order for localized product names. Repro steps:
1) Add products named 'Åke' and 'Apple' in Swedish locale.
2) Search for 'A' and expect Åke to appear alongside Apple according to Swedish collation, but Åke shows after Z.

SQLite query log:
SELECT name FROM products WHERE name LIKE 'A%' ORDER BY name COLLATE NOCASE;

Stack trace:
#0 0x00007f8b2 in sqlite3_exec
#1 0x0000555555 in DB::query
#2 0x0000555512 in SearchEngine::search

C++ code (DB.cpp):

void DB::open(const std::string& path) {
    sqlite3_open(path.c_str(), &db_);
    // current code sets default collation to NOCASE only
}

std::vector<std::string> SearchEngine::search(const std::string& q) {
    std::string sql = "SELECT name FROM products WHERE name LIKE ? ORDER BY name COLLATE NOCASE";
    // bind and execute
}

We must integrate ICU collations with SQLite (using ICU extension) and register a collation for current locale, then use ORDER BY name COLLATE ICU_LOCALE. Add migration step for existing DBs and tests for sv_SE collation. Also update CMakeLists.txt to link -licuuc -licuio and provide preprocessor guards for platforms that don't support ICU.

LONG: Localization keys mismatch causing crash during startup. Error log:
[ERROR] LocalizationManager: key 'welcome_message_title' not found in bundle 'fr'. Throwing exception.
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'
  what():  Missing translation: welcome_message_title
Aborted (core dumped)

Code excerpt (LocalizationManager.cpp):

const std::string& LocalizationManager::get(const std::string& key) {
    auto it = bundle_.find(key);
    if (it == bundle_.end()) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Missing translation: " + key);
    }
    return it->second; // returns reference to value in temporary? maybe safe
}

Repro: Set device locale to French and launch app; crash occurs before UI shows. Fix suggestions: replace throwing with fallback to base locale or return placeholder (e.g., "[missing: key]"). Also implement a preflight check at startup to log missing keys per bundle instead of crashing, and create a release-time build flag to fail-fast only in CI. Please implement changes and add unit tests that simulate missing keys and confirm graceful degradation.

LONG: Android JNI bridging of localized strings is leaking memory and causing corrupted characters. Stack trace (art):
F/libc    : Fatal signal 11 (SIGSEGV), code 1, fault addr 0x8 in tid 2000
Backtrace:
  #00 pc 0001b2cc  /system/lib/libart.so
  #01 pc 0000ab34  /data/app/.../libnative-lib.so (Java_com_example_Localization_getString+52)

JNI method (Localization_jni.cpp):

extern "C" JNIEXPORT jstring JNICALL
Java_com_example_Localization_getString(JNIEnv* env, jobject, jstring key) {
    const char* k = env->GetStringUTFChars(key, nullptr);
    std::string res = LocalizationManager::instance().get(k);
    env->ReleaseStringUTFChars(key, k);
    return env->NewStringUTF(res.c_str());
}

Problems:
- GetStringUTFChars expects modified UTF-8; NewStringUTF may fail for embedded nulls or non-BMP characters.
- Potential lifetime issues if res contains invalid UTF-8.
- No exception checks for missing keys.

Request: Change to NewStringUTF if safe, otherwise use NewStringUTF with proper conversion (jchar array via NewString), and ensure we catch exceptions from C++ layer and return a safe placeholder. Add tests for keys containing emoji and CJK characters. Also fix memory leak by ensuring we always ReleaseStringUTFChars on all code paths.

LONG: Plurals implemented poorly causing wrong grammar in Serbian. Reproduction:
- In chat, unread message count shows '1 poruka', '2 poruka', '5 poruka' but correct forms are '1 poruka', '2 poruke', '5 poruka'.

Current code (PluralHandler.cpp):

std::string PluralHandler::format(const std::string& key, int n) {
    if (n==1) return get(key + "_one");
    return get(key + "_other");
}

We need full CLDR plural rules or integrate a plural library (e.g., ICU message format or CLDR plural rules). Implement support for multiple plural forms (zero, one, two, few, many, other) and update translator pipeline to produce resource entries for each form (key_one, key_few, etc.). Add sample Serbian rules in tests and migrate existing translations. Also check UI layout for wide plural variants.

LONG: Database charset mismatches cause mojibake when saving localized user bios. Repro steps:
1) Set device to Chinese Simplified.
2) Save profile bio with Chinese characters.
3) On reload, content shows as garbled text.

Observed SQL trace:
INSERT INTO user_profiles (bio) VALUES ('åäö...')

Code (DBAdapter.cpp):

void DBAdapter::open() {
    sqlite3_open(dbPath.c_str(), &db_);
    // missing PRAGMA encoding = "UTF-8"; and not setting text encoding on windows
}

char* DBAdapter::escape(const std::string& s) {
    // current escape assumes ASCII and may truncate on null bytes
}

Fix: ensure DB opened with UTF-8 encoding and use sqlite3_bind_text(..., SQLITE_TRANSIENT) with proper byte lengths. Add tests for insert/retrieve of multi-byte strings and migration tool to fix any corrupted records. Also add logging to identify affected user IDs.

LONG: TextInput boundary issues for combining characters causing caret to jump mid-cluster in Indonesian input. Repro steps:
1) Open message composer in indonesian locale.
2) Type characters with diacritics using combining sequences.
3) Move caret; it sometimes splits combined grapheme.

Relevant code (TextInput.cpp):

size_t TextInput::nextGrapheme(size_t pos) {
    // naive check for UTF-8 continuation bytes
    while (pos < text_.size() && (text_[pos] & 0xC0) == 0x80) pos++;
    return pos+1;
}

This logic doesn't handle Unicode combining marks, ZWJ, regional indicators. Request: integrate ICU's break iterator (ubrk_open(UBRK_CHARACTER,...)) to compute grapheme clusters properly. Provide update to TextInput::nextGrapheme using UBreakIterator and add tests for emoji sequences, Thai, and Indic scripts.

LONG: Crash on app start for Turkish locales due to incorrect tolower/toupper usage on UTF-8; reproduction:
1) Set device language to Turkish (tr-TR).
2) Launch app.
3) Crash with std::out_of_range in string operations.

Log excerpt:
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::out_of_range'
  what():  basic_string::erase
#0  0x0000555555 in main
#1  0x00007ffff7a334: __libc_start_main

Code (StringUtils.cpp):

std::string toLowerAscii(std::string s) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
        s[i] = std::tolower(s[i]); // assumes ASCII
    }
    return s;
}

std::string localeCompareKey(const std::string& s) {
    auto k = toLowerAscii(s);
    k.erase(std::remove_if(k.begin(), k.end(), isspace), k.end());
    return k;
}

Problems:
- Using tolower on signed char may be undefined for non-ASCII
- Turkish dotted/dotless i rules break ASCII-only approach
- erase with isspace (int) predicate wrong uses causing UB

Request: Use ICU UnicodeString for case folding with locale: UnicodeString u(s.c_str(), "UTF-8"); u.toLower(locale); return u.toUTF8String(); Also fix predicate usage and add unit tests for Turkish 'I'/'i' cases and non-ASCII inputs.

LONG: Slow startup when loading many locale bundles; profiler shows heavy CPU in parsing JSON for translations. Example stack:
  ResourceLoader::parseJson -> rapidjson::Document::Parse -> cpu hot

Current code (ResourceLoader.cpp):

void ResourceLoader::loadBundleSync(const std::string& tag) {
    auto path = bundlePath(tag);
    std::ifstream f(path);
    std::string s((std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(f)), std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
    rapidjson::Document d;
    d.Parse(s.c_str()); // blocking
    bundles_[tag] = std::move(d);
}

Request: Move heavy parsing off main thread, parse into a compact binary format (e.g., serialized protobuf or pre-compiled binary table) at build time, and provide asynchronous loader with callback to apply. Provide fallback to on-demand JSON for languages added at runtime. Add benchmark and ensure parse latency <50ms per bundle on low-end devices. Update CMake toolchain to produce binary bundle artifacts.

LONG: Missing support for locale-aware sorting in LDAP search integration; user names with diacritics appear in wrong order when synced to contact list. Repro:
1) Sync LDAP directory with names 'Émile', 'Emily', 'Andre'.
2) In fr_FR locale, expected order: 'Andre', 'Émile', 'Emily' (accent-aware) but current order is ASCII-sorted making 'Émile' last.

Sync code (LDAPSync.cpp):

std::sort(users.begin(), users.end(), [](const User& a, const User& b){ return a.name < b.name; });

Need: Use ICU collator:

UErrorCode status = U_ZERO_ERROR;
UCollator* coll = ucol_open("fr_FR", &status);
std::sort(users.begin(), users.end(), [&](const User& a, const User& b){
    UChar ua[256], ub[256];
    u_strFromUTF8(ua, 256, NULL, a.name.c_str(), -1, &status);
    u_strFromUTF8(ub, 256, NULL, b.name.c_str(), -1, &status);
    return ucol_strcoll(coll, ua, -1, ub, -1) == UCOL_LESS;
});
ucol_close(coll);

Please implement collator-based sorting, add caching of collator per-locale, and ensure thread-safety. Add unit tests for fr_FR and sv_SE ordering and update LDAP sync migration notes.

LONG: Fuzzy matching for locale negotiation fails with custom tags. Repro steps:
1) Device locale set to 'es-419' (Latin America Spanish).
2) App supports 'es', 'es-MX', 'es-ES'. Negotiation chooses 'es-ES'.

Code (LocaleMatcher.cpp):

std::string LocaleMatcher::bestMatch(const std::string& requested) {
    if (supported_.count(requested)) return requested;
    auto lang = requested.substr(0, requested.find('-'));
    if (supported_.count(lang)) return lang;
    return "en";
}

Problem: 'es-419' should prefer 'es' or 'es-MX' depending on region mapping. Request: implement CLDR likely-subtags or use BCP47 matching algorithm to choose closest supported locale, mapping 'es-419' -> 'es' or choose region mapping table for Latin America prefer 'es-419' -> 'es-MX' if available. Add unit tests for 'pt-BR', 'zh-Hant-HK', 'es-419' and provide fallback ordering. Update code to expose matched tag for telemetry.

LONG: In-app help content is localized using HTML stored in SQLite; some locales include right-to-left content which renders incorrectly because our WebView is forced LTR. Repro:
1) Open Help, switch to Arabic.
2) Text aligns left and punctuation order is wrong.

Database row for help page contains: lang='ar', content='<p>نص بالعربية</p>'

C++ WebView bridge (WebViewBridge.cpp):

void WebViewBridge::loadHtml(const std::string& html) {
    std::string wrapped = "<html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"></head><body>" + html + "</body></html>";
    nativeWebView_->loadData(wrapped);
}

Fix: Add dir="rtl" on html/body when locale is RTL. Also ensure <meta name="viewport"> and proper dir and lang attributes set (<html lang="ar" dir="rtl">). Update content sanitizer to not strip dir/lang. Provide code changes and add test pages verifying mirroring, punctuation, and bidi ordering.

LONG: Translation extraction misses strings built via fmt::format where format key is assembled at runtime, causing untranslated text in production. Example code:

std::string label = fmt::format("menu.{}", viewType);
UIButton->setTitle(I18N(label));

Extractor (xgettext wrapper) can't find 'menu.{}' since it's dynamic.

Request:
1) Audit codebase for dynamic key construction and replace with enum-to-key mapping or centralized function getMenuKey(viewType) that returns compile-time constants.
2) For unavoidable dynamic keys, ensure a developer-maintained manifest lists possible variants for extraction.
3) Update extraction tooling to parse fmt::format patterns where the first argument is a literal.

Add example change: replace above with

switch(viewType) {
 case ViewType::Home: key = "menu.home"; break;
 case ViewType::Settings: key = "menu.settings"; break;
}
UIButton->setTitle(I18N(key));

Include guidance for translators and add unit tests ensuring all keys from enum are present in PO files.

Long: Persistent refresh token not persisted correctly across CLI restarts. Repro steps:
1) zig-cli login --browser
2) Close terminal, reopen
3) Run zig-cli status -> it asks to login again

Observed logs:
ERROR: auth: token_store: failed to write credentials: OsError{ code: 13, msg: "Permission denied" }

Relevant Zig snippet from token store:

const std = @import("std");
fn saveToken(allocator: *std.mem.Allocator, tokenJson: []const u8) !void {
    var f = try std.fs.cwd().createFile(".config/zig-cli/credentials", .{});
    defer f.close();
    try f.writeAll(tokenJson);
}

We need to use std.fs.homeDir() and proper permissions (0600), ensure directories are created, and handle Windows path join. Also catch and suggest chmod for permission denied errors.

Long: Token verification fails for ES512-signed tokens. Example stack trace when validating signature locally:

err: SignatureMismatch
stack:
  at .verifyToken (src/auth/verify.zig:72)
  at .main (src/cli.zig:120)

Code excerpt:

const std = @import("std");
pub fn verifyToken(alloc: *std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator, token: []const u8, jwk: Jwk) !bool {
    var parts = try std.mem.split(token, '.');
    if (parts.len != 3) return error.InvalidToken;
    // verify using jwk.alg
    switch (jwk.alg) {
        "ES256" => // ok
        "ES384" => // ok
        else => return error.UnsupportedAlg;
    }
}

We need to add ES512 support and ensure signature verification uses correct curve parameters with std.crypto. Also log which alg was used in failure messages.

Long: Reproduce: login hangs forever in headless Docker container. Logs:

2025-10-01T12:00:00Z INFO auth: starting browser flow
2025-10-01T12:00:10Z WARN auth: failed to open browser: spawn /usr/bin/xdg-open: no such file
2025-10-01T12:10:00Z ERROR auth: browser flow timeout

Code in login.zig:

pub fn startBrowserFlow() !void {
    const cmd = try std.process.Command.init(allocator, "xdg-open");
    try cmd.spawn();
    // poll for token
}

Fix: detect missing GUI and auto-switch to device code flow, with clear output. Also set shorter default timeout in CI (30s) and allow override with --browser-timeout.

Long: When verifying access against the REST API, the CLI incorrectly caches denied permissions, causing a user to be permanently blocked until cache TTL expires. Repro:
1) revoke permission on server
2) run 'zig-cli action' -> 403 saved
3) re-grant permission server-side
4) 'zig-cli action' still fails until timeout

Relevant caching code:

var permCache: std.AutoHashMap([]const u8, bool) = undefined;

pub fn hasPermission(name: []const u8) bool {
    const cached = permCache.get(name);
    if (cached) return cached.*;
    const allowed = checkServer(name);
    permCache.put(name, allowed);
    return allowed;
}

We need to add cache invalidation on any successful auth refresh or explicit 'zig-cli auth invalidate-cache' command and define TTL. Also persist cache version tied to server policy_version.

Long: Audit log missing principal info on service-account actions. Example audit entry:

{"time":"...","action":"deploy","principal":null,"resource":"cluster-1"}

We want to include service_account_id and human-readable name. CLI code that signs requests currently sets header 'X-Caller' only for user tokens, not for service account keys.

Snippet:

if (isUserToken(token)) {
    req.headers.put("X-Caller", username);
}

Modify to extract 'sub' from JWT or 'kid' from key and set both X-Caller-ID and X-Caller-Type, and add unit tests verifying headers for user vs service account.

Long: Add support for SCIM provisioning via CLI for enterprise customers. Requirements:
- 'zig-cli scim create-user --username bob --email bob@example.com --roles viewer'
- Map CLI flags to SCIM schema and call /scim/v2/Users with Bearer token

Example Zig HTTP call:

const client = std.http.Client.init(allocator, allocator);
var req = client.request(.POST, scimUrl) catch unreachable;
req.setHeader("Authorization", &.{ 'B', 'e', 'a', 'r', 'e', 'r', ' ', token });
const body = try std.json.stringify(allocator, userObj);
req.setBody(body);
const resp = try req.send();

Add idempotency handling on 409 conflict (user exists) and map response to friendly output. Include tests for 201, 400, 409 errors.

Long: Certificate pinning for auth server fails with dynamic leaf rotation; the CLI rejects rotated certs with the error:

error: certificate pinning failed: no match
stack:
  at checkServerCert (src/net/tls.zig:48)

Pinning code:

const expected = "sha256/abcd...";
const got = try std.crypto.sha256Alloc(allocator, cert.der);
if (std.mem.eql(u8, got, expected) == false) return error.PinMismatch;

Improve by pinning public key instead of entire cert, support multiple pins (backup keys), and allow 'pin-mode' config with values enforce/report-only. Add migration scripts to accept legacy pins.

Long: CLI's role assumption uses STS-like AssumeRole that returns temporary credentials. The issue: these credentials are not used for subsequent HTTPS requests because HTTP client uses cached transport with TLS session bound to previous certificate, causing 401s. Logs:

WARN: http: using stale transport after credential rotation
ERROR: api: 401 Unauthorized

We need to flush HTTP client pool and rebuild client with new Authorization header. Example correction:

pub fn rotateCreds(newToken: []const u8) void {
    allocator.deinitTransport(&httpClient);
    httpClient = try createClient(allocator, newToken);
}

Also add unit test that simulates token rotate and ensures next request sends new token.

Long: Security bug: CLI accepts an auth config file pointed via --auth-config but doesn't validate file permissions, allowing world-readable credentials when run by root. Add check in config loader to validate file mode <= 0600 and warn or refuse to load if too permissive. Provide explicit error message with 'chmod 600 <file>' suggestion. Code example:

const stat = try fs.stat(authPath);
if ((stat.mode & 0o077) != 0) return error.PermissionTooOpen;

Add automated test on Unix and skip on Windows.

Long: Add support for signed JWT assertion grant for OAuth2 service-to-service authentication. The CLI should construct JWT with claims iss, sub, aud, iat, exp, sign with ES256 using local key, then POST to /oauth/token with grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer and assertion=<jwt>. Example Zig signing code:

var signer = try std.crypto.EcdsaSigner(.P256, allocator, private_key_der);
const jwt = try jwtBuild(allocator, claims);
const sig = try signer.sign(jwt);
const assertion = try encodeJwt(jwt, sig);

Add flags: --service-account-key, --token-audience, and tests verifying successful token exchange and error messages on invalid signature.

Long: Implement key rotation command 'zig-cli key rotate --kid <kid>' that uploads a new public key to server, waits for propagation, then marks old key as retiring but still accepts it for 1 hour. Show CLI output and server poll. Include sample Zig code to generate ES256 key with std.crypto and write PEM:

var pkey = try std.crypto.generateEccKeypair(.P256, allocator);
const pem = try pkey.toPem(allocator);
try fs.createFile(....).writeAll(pem);

Add flags --grace-period and safety checks to prevent accidental lockout.

Long: The CLI's DB-backed session store (SQLite) has mis-specified schema for storing token_scopes as TEXT but tries to use JSON arrays leading to sqlite error: 'datatype mismatch'. Migration required to change column to JSON (or TEXT but serialize correctly). Current schema:

CREATE TABLE sessions (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, token TEXT, token_scopes JSON);

SQLite doesn't enforce JSON type by default; migration should ALTER TABLE using temp table copy, or add code to serialize scopes into comma-separated string and update code to parse it back. Provide migration SQL and Zig code for reading/writing scopes safely.

Long: Security improvement: add signature verification for any server-sent policy files before applying them locally. When fetching policy.json, verify signed JWS envelope using server's known policy-signing key. Example Zig snippet to verify JWS compact:

const parts = std.mem.split(jws, '.');
// verify header.payload signature

If signature invalid, refuse to apply policy and log details. Add fallback to manual admin approval mode.

Long: Disk cache performance regression: writing many small files used to be fast but now is slow. Profiler points to FileChannel.transferTo when moving temp files. Stack snippet:
at sun.nio.ch.FileChannelImpl.transferTo(FileChannelImpl.java:856)
at com.app.cache.DiskCache.atomicReplace(DiskCache.java:210)

Current write code:
Path temp = Files.createTempFile(cacheDir, "tmp", null);
Files.write(temp, data);
Files.move(temp, target, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE);

We should batch writes, reduce syncs by using write with StandardOpenOption.SYNC false, or use a single per-cache writer thread to serialize disk I/O. Also add monitoring to detect slow disk and degrade behavior (compress, reduce writes).

Long: Cache compute race with computeIfAbsent results in duplicate network calls. Example:

// simplified
public CompletableFuture<Data> get(String key) {
    return cache.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> fetchFromNetwork(k));
}

But fetchFromNetwork returns CompletableFuture and computeIfAbsent runs mapping function synchronously leading to duplicate calls when multiple threads concurrently call get() because mapping function invoked multiple times due to absent mapping before insertion. Observed logs:
2025-11-01 11:10:01 INFO Network - fetching key=abc
2025-11-01 11:10:01 INFO Network - fetching key=abc

Proposed: use a ConcurrentHashMap<String, CompletableFuture<Data>> and computeIfAbsent with a supplier that creates and puts the same CompletableFuture, or use Guava's LoadingCache with asynchronous reload. Add tests to verify only one fetch per key.

Long: Detailed bug — thumbnails show for wrong files after renaming in-place. Repro steps:
1) Open folder and let thumbnails generate
2) Rename file A.png -> B.png in the OS
3) Refresh folder in app
Observed: B.png shows A.png's thumbnail. Code snippet:
String key = file.getName();
Thumbnail t = cache.get(key);
if (t != null && t.lastModified == file.lastModified()) return t.image;

Problem: key uses filename only; lastModified comparison can collide because some filesystems have 1s resolution. Fix by using fileKey = Files.getAttribute(path, "unix:ino") or compute content hash on write. If unavailable, combine size+mtime with fallback to full hash for safety. Add tests on Windows and macOS.

Long: Report — profile shows frequent blocking on synchronized cache get when many threads request different keys. Traces point to synchronized methods in LegacyCache.java. Example stack snapshot:
Thread-1 -> LegacyCache.get -> synchronized(legacyCache) { ... }
Thread-2 -> LegacyCache.get -> waiting for monitor

LegacyCache code:
public synchronized V get(K key) {
    // heavy computation when miss
}

Propose refactor: remove coarse-grained synchronization and use ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> with computeIfAbsent to allow concurrent reads and per-key computation. For long-running load operations, return Futures so threads can wait without locking the map. Add benchmark comparing old vs new under 64 concurrent threads.

Long: During start, cache loader spawns too many threads for warming resulting in 'Too many open files' and failing to open sockets. Error:
java.io.IOException: Too many open files
	at java.io.FileInputStream.open0(Native Method)
	at java.io.FileInputStream.open(FileInputStream.java:195)

Warm-up code:
for (Path p : filesToWarm) {
    executor.submit(() -> load(p));
}

We need to cap concurrency (FixedThreadPool with size tuned to disk and CPU), use Semaphore to bound open file descriptors, and throttle warm-up. Also implement backpressure: if file open fails, re-queue with exponential backoff and increase logging to surface the root cause to users.

Long: Need a deterministically testable disk cache with injection points for time and storage. Currently many tests are flaky because eviction is time-based and uses System.currentTimeMillis. Suggest injecting Clock interface and an in-memory FileSystem (Jimfs) for unit tests. Example code change:
public DiskCache(Path dir, Clock clock) {
    this.dir = dir;
    this.clock = clock;
}

Then tests can simulate TTL expiry by advancing a fake clock and assert eviction happens. Also add integration tests for atomic writes using Jimfs to verify atomic move behavior.

Long: The preferences cache leaks when listeners are registered but not unregistered, preventing GC of Preference objects. Repro: repeatedly open/close projects and observe heap growth in MAT showing retained Preference instances with references from PreferenceChangeSupport. Fix: use WeakListeners or ensure listeners are removed in project.close(). Provide code example:
preferenceNode.removePreferenceChangeListener(myListener);

Add lifecycle checks to clear caches and listeners on project unload and tests that mimic open/close cycles.

Long: Provide a migration tool and code snippet to convert existing SerializedObjectCache to JSONFileCache. Example migration code:
Path oldDir = Paths.get(oldCache);
Path newDir = Paths.get(newCache);
for (Path file : Files.newDirectoryStream(oldDir)) {
    try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(Files.newInputStream(file))) {
        Object obj = ois.readObject();
        String json = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(obj);
        Files.write(newDir.resolve(file.getFileName().toString()+".json"), json.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    } catch (Exception e) {
        logger.warn("Skipping corrupted cache file {}", file, e);
    }
}

This should be run on first startup after upgrade and respect a dry-run flag so admins can audit conversions.

When using the SDK inside a Flask app, request context can leak into background threads. Reproduce:

app = Flask(__name__)
client = Client()  # global

@app.route('/start')
def start():
    def worker():
        client.create_job({'type': 'heavy'})
    Thread(target=worker).start()
    return 'started'

We see RuntimeError: Working outside of request context in logs. Add guidance and an explicit thread-safe client factory or a context manager for Flask/Gunicorn thread pools.

Add a pluggable serializer API so SDK can support XML or Protobuf for certain endpoints. Include sample implementation for protobuf:

class ProtobufSerializer(Serializer):
    def dumps(self, obj):
        return obj.SerializeToString()
    def loads(self, data):
        msg = MyProtoMessage()
        msg.ParseFromString(data)
        return msg

And add a client option transport_serializer=ProtobufSerializer() which sets Content-Type and Accept headers accordingly. Provide docs and an end-to-end test that posts and receives a proto message.

Add support for per-request custom timeouts and a guard that raises RequestTimeoutError with elapsed time included. Example:

try:
    client.get_big_report(timeout=1.0)
except RequestTimeoutError as e:
    logger.info('timed out after %s', e.elapsed)

Add an example showing how to use the SDK to migrate user data into the service with chunked uploads, error handling, resume capability, and progress reporting. Include sample code:

from my_sdk import Client

client = Client()
with open('users.ndjson') as f:
    for chunk in chunked(f, 1000):
        try:
            client.users.bulk_create(chunk)
        except RateLimitError as e:
            sleep(e.retry_after)
            retry

Long: When deserializing nested objects, we loose original unknown fields. Example api response:

{
  "id": "r1",
  "name": "test",
  "meta": {"x-feature": true, "new_field": "value"}
}

Our model currently drops 'new_field' during parsing. Request: implement preservation of unknown fields (e.g., model._extra) and include round-trip serialization so unknown fields are not lost. Provide code example:

resp = client.resources.get('r1')
print(resp._extra['meta']['new_field'])

Also include tests that ensure unknown fields survive parse->serialize->send cycles.

Long: Unhandled rejection in API client when server returns 202 with empty body

We're seeing unhandled promise rejections in the API client when a request resolves with HTTP 202 Accepted and an empty body. The client calls response.json() without checking Content-Length or status code. In Node fetch polyfill response.json() throws SyntaxError on empty body, causing an unhandled rejection.

Error log:

(node:12345) UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning: SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input
    at JSON.parse (<anonymous>)
    at /node_modules/node-fetch/lib/body.js:xx:xx
    at processTicksAndRejections (internal/process/task_queues.js:93:5)

Code path:

async function request(url, options) {
  const res = await fetch(url, options);
  const data = await res.json(); // <-- fails on empty body
  if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status, data);
  return data;
}

Suggested fix: If status === 204 || res.headers.get('content-length') === '0' || res.headers.get('content-type') is null, return null instead of calling res.json(). Alternatively, use res.text() then parse if non-empty.

Please add tests simulating 202 with empty body and ensure no unhandled rejections.

Long: Memory leak in subscription manager after hot module replacement (HMR)

Description:
After enabling HMR in development, each hot-reload causes subscriptions created by useSubscription hook to accumulate in SubscriptionManager._listeners. Over time the array grows and never releases old listener references leading to high memory usage.

Reproduction:
- Start dev server with HMR
- Open app and navigate to page using useSubscription
- Save file to trigger HMR 10+ times
- Observe process memory keeps increasing and listeners count grows

Stack trace (when inspecting heap):

SubscriptionManager.addListener @ lib/subscription.js:34
useSubscription @ lib/hooks/useSubscription.js:12
App.render @ src/App.js:45

Relevant code:

class SubscriptionManager {
  constructor() { this._listeners = []; }
  addListener(listener) { this._listeners.push(listener); }
  removeListener(listener) { // currently uses filter which fails when listener identity changes
    this._listeners = this._listeners.filter(l => l !== listener);
  }
}

function useSubscription(topic) {
  const manager = useContext(SubscriptionContext);
  useEffect(() => {
    const listener = data => setState(data);
    manager.addListener(listener);
    return () => manager.removeListener(listener);
  }, [topic]);
}

Hypothesis: HMR wraps functions, changing identity so removeListener doesn't find the original listener. Fix options:
- Use a Set instead of array and store a stable ID (e.g., Symbol) on listener so remove works across HMR
- Or weak references (WeakSet) where possible

Please implement Set-based listener storage, add tests simulating HMR wrapping by creating equivalent but not identical listener functions and asserting removal works, and add a leak regression test.

Long: Plugin loader fails with ESM default export when consuming with require()

Symptoms:
When loading third-party plugins that are authored as ES Modules and compiled to have default export (e.g., export default function plugin() {}), the plugin loader fails with "plugin is not a function" because require() returns { default: [Function] } and our loader calls pluginFunc.apply(...).

Error message:

TypeError: plugin is not a function
    at loadPlugin (lib/plugins/loader.js:52:17)
    at initialize (lib/app/init.js:34:12)

Plugin loader snippet:

function loadPlugin(path) {
  const plugin = require(path);
  plugin(app);
}

Proposed fix: normalize the exported module to accept both CommonJS and ESM default exports. Example:

function unwrapModule(mod) {
  return (mod && mod.__esModule && mod.default) ? mod.default : mod;
}

Then call const plugin = unwrapModule(require(path));

Also update documentation and add tests for both module styles.

Long: JSONP callback name collision causing cross-request data leak

We use JSONP fallback for older browsers. The callback name generator uses a monotonically increasing counter stored globally: __jsonp_cb1, __jsonp_cb2, etc. In single-page apps with long lifetimes, when multiple requests are made and older callbacks are not cleaned, new callbacks can accidentally invoke stale handlers, leaking previous response data into new requests.

Error reproduction:
- Make a JSONP request that never cleans up (simulate network drop) so window.__jsonp_cb3 remains defined
- Later make a new request that gets JSONP response calling __jsonp_cb3, the old handler executes with a different promise resolver and cause incorrect promise resolution

Current generator code:

let cbId = 0;
function jsonp(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const name = `__jsonp_cb${++cbId}`;
    window[name] = data => { resolve(data); };
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = `${url}?callback=${name}`;
    document.head.appendChild(script);
  });
}

Issues:
- No cleanup of window[name] and script tag
- Global counter may reuse names if page reloads or modules reinitialized

Fixes suggested:
- Ensure window[name] is deleted after invocation and when timeout/reject happens
- Use Symbol-like unique names (timestamp + random) to reduce collisions
- Add timeout to reject and cleanup on network failure

Please implement cleanup and add integration tests simulating late JSONP callbacks and ensuring they don't resolve stale promises.

Long: 'Invalid character' error when parsing YAML config in Node 14 due to BOM

On startup, parsing YAML config files sometimes fails with YamlException: Bad indentation or Invalid character at line 1 column 1. Investigation shows files created on Windows include a UTF-8 BOM (0xEF 0xBB 0xBF) which js-yaml doesn't strip before parsing in our wrapper.

Error stack:

YamlException: bad indentation of a mapping entry at line 1, column 1:
    �plugins: []
    ^
    at generateError (node_modules/js-yaml/lib/js-yaml/loader.js:162:10)

Current code:

const fs = require('fs');
const yaml = require('js-yaml');

function loadConfig(path) {
  const raw = fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf8');
  return yaml.load(raw);
}

Fix: strip BOM before parsing:

const rawSanitized = raw.replace(/^\uFEFF/, '');

Also add tests for files starting with BOM and ensure config loads. Consider using fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf8').replace(/^\uFEFF/, '') centrally in config loader.

Long: GraphQL client query batching drops variables for later queries

We implemented query batching that groups queries within the same tick and sends them as an array to /graphql/batch. However, variables for subsequent queries were being mutated because we reused the same variables object reference and then cleared it after sending, leading to empty variables on the server for queries 2..n.

Repro steps:
1. Send two queries in quick succession using client.query(q1, vars1) and client.query(q2, vars2)
2. Server receives batch where only first item has variables; others have {}

Client batching code snippet:

function enqueue(query, variables) {
  batch.push({ query, variables });
  setTimeout(flushBatch, 0);
}

function flushBatch() {
  const payload = JSON.stringify(batch);
  batch.length = 0;
  fetch('/graphql/batch', { method: 'POST', body: payload });
}

Bug: the same variables object gets reused by callers when using spread or mutation upstream. Also our code should deepClone variables per queued item to be safe. Additionally, JSON.stringify on batch after clearing the array (if asynchronous) could be affected. Better to copy batch into local var: const toSend = batch.slice(); batch.length = 0; fetch(..., JSON.stringify(toSend)).

Please implement deep-clone (structuredClone or JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())) for queued variables and ensure atomic flush with local copy. Add test that mocks two queries with distinct variables and asserts server receives both correctly.

Long: Backend adapter throws 'RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded' on circular JSON

The library's default logging serializer calls JSON.stringify on arbitrary objects. When a user logs an object containing circular references (common in debugging DOM nodes or parent/child pointers), it causes RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded crashing the process.

Stack trace:

RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded
    at JSON.stringify (<anonymous>)
    at safeSerialize (lib/utils/serialize.js:12:15)
    at Logger.log (lib/logger.js:34:12)

Current safeSerialize implementation:

function safeSerialize(obj) {
  try { return JSON.stringify(obj); }
  catch (e) { return String(obj); }
}

Proposed fix: implement a replacer that tracks seen objects and replaces circular refs with [Circular]. Example:

function safeSerialize(obj) {
  const seen = new WeakSet();
  return JSON.stringify(obj, function (key, value) {
    if (value && typeof value === 'object') {
      if (seen.has(value)) return '[Circular]';
      seen.add(value);
    }
    return value;
  });
}

Add unit tests for objects with circular refs and for DOM nodes that contain cycles. Ensure performance remains acceptable and consider fallback for non-WeakSet environments.

Long: HTTP client fails to follow 307 redirect for POST requests

Issue:
When server responds 307 Temporary Redirect to a POST request, fetch should re-POST to redirected URL. Our wrapper normalizes to GET for second request resulting in data loss and unexpected server errors.

Observed behavior with logs:
Request: POST /api/upload
Response: 307 Location: /v2/upload
Follow-up request: GET /v2/upload (no body)
Server returns 405 Method Not Allowed

Client wrapper code:

async function fetchWithRedirect(url, options) {
  const res = await fetch(url, options);
  if (res.status >= 300 && res.status < 400 && res.headers.get('location')) {
    return fetch(res.headers.get('location'), {}); // <-- drops method and body
  }
  return res;
}

Fix: honor redirect status codes (301,302,307,308) semantics — for 307/308 retain method and body; for 301/302, follow browser default (usually change to GET for POST), but better to pass through method unless explicitly desired. Use original options for 307/308.

Also add tests covering 301/302/307/308 and ensure POST bodies are preserved for 307/308. Update docs to specify redirect behavior.

Long: Auto-formatting JSON editor corrupts large files (>1MB) causing the UI to freeze

Symptom:
When user pastes a large JSON payload into the in-browser JSON editor, the auto-format step (which pretty-prints and highlights) blocks the main thread and sometimes causes the tab to become unresponsive for several seconds. In some cases, the browser kills the script.

Relevant code:

function formatAndHighlight(text) {
  const obj = JSON.parse(text);
  const pretty = JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2);
  editor.setValue(pretty);
  highlight(pretty);
}

Problems:
- Synchronous JSON.parse and stringify for huge payloads is expensive
- highlight() runs tokenization on main thread

Proposed fixes:
- Offload parsing/stringify and syntax highlighting to Web Worker for payloads > 200KB
- Implement incremental/streaming tokenization in worker
- While worker is running show a spinner and throttle user edits

Please implement worker-based formatting with a fallback to sync behavior for small files. Add automated performance tests to ensure formatting of a 5MB JSON completes within 1s on modern hardware and doesn't block UI.

Long: getConfig() returns stale values under concurrent modifications

We expose a getConfig() accessor that returns a reference to internal config object. When external code mutates returned object, internal state is silently changed leading to race conditions when multiple callers adjust settings concurrently.

Example reproducer:

const cfg1 = lib.getConfig();
cfg1.timeout = 1000; // mutates internal state
// another module also does const cfg2 = lib.getConfig(); cfg2.retry = 0;

Root cause: returning the internal object by reference rather than a defensive copy.

Fix: make getConfig() return a deep-cloned object or an immutable shallow copy (Object.freeze) depending on size. Also provide setConfig() to update config through a controlled API that merges changes and emits change events.

Suggested API:

function getConfig() { return deepClone(internalConfig); }
function setConfig(partial) { internalConfig = merge(internalConfig, partial); emit('config:changed', internalConfig); }

Add tests verifying original internalConfig cannot be mutated via getConfig() return value and that setConfig merges correctly.

Long: Refactor the user registration flow — it's brittle and mixes validation, hashing, and DB calls. Current code in authController.ts:

async function register(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  if (!email || !password) return res.status(400).json({ message: 'Missing fields' });
  const hashed = bcrypt.hashSync(password, 10);
  const user = await db.query(`INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *`, [email, hashed]);
  // send welcome email (inline)
  sendMail(email, 'Welcome');
  res.status(201).json(user.rows[0]);
}

Issues: sync hashing blocks event loop, validation is ad-hoc, DB query uses string SQL in controller, email sending is inline and unrecoverable. Please refactor to:
- Use async bcrypt.hash
- Add Zod schema for registration DTO
- Move DB insert into UserRepository.create(userDto)
- Inject EmailService and enqueue welcome email (non-blocking)
- Return typed UserDto and handle errors with ApiError
Provide updated TypeScript code and unit tests.

Long: We're getting intermittent errors in the product search endpoint with stack trace:

TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toLowerCase')
    at SearchService.filter (/srv/api/src/services/search.service.ts:82:24)
    at processTicksAndRejections (node:internal/process/task_queues:96:5)
    at SearchController.search (/srv/api/src/controllers/search.controller.ts:34:7)

Repro: Call /api/search?q=<empty-string-or-missing> or q param with non-string. Current code assumes q is string:

// search.service.ts
export function filter(query: string, items: Item[]): Item[] {
  const q = query.toLowerCase().trim();
  return items.filter(i => i.name.toLowerCase().includes(q));
}

Refactor to validate input types and sanitize. Accept optional q, default to '' and ensure items have safe string checks. Update controller signature to expect SearchQuery DTO: { q?: string; page?: number } and add unit tests covering undefined/malformed inputs.

Long: Failure in TypeORM migrations when enabling strict mode. Error:

TS2322: Type '(entity: any) => Promise<void>' is not assignable to type 'MigrationInterface.up'.
  Type 'Promise<void>' is missing the following properties from type 'Promise<any>': then, catch, finally

Context: We have migrations written like:

export default {
  async up(queryRunner) {
    await queryRunner.query('ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN bio text');
  }
}

But TypeScript expects the class-based MigrationInterface. Refactor migrations to typed classes and enable tsconfig strict options. Example:

import { MigrationInterface, QueryRunner } from 'typeorm';

export class AddBioToUsers1670000000000 implements MigrationInterface {
  public async up(queryRunner: QueryRunner): Promise<void> {
    await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN bio text`);
  }
  public async down(queryRunner: QueryRunner): Promise<void> {
    await queryRunner.query(`ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN bio`);
  }
}

Please update all migrations and adjust ormconfig to load compiled JS or enable ts-node for migrations.

Long: Intermittent 500s in webhook receiver with stack trace:

Error: Request failed with status code 400
    at createError (/srv/api/node_modules/axios/lib/core/createError.js:16:15)
    at settle (/srv/api/node_modules/axios/lib/core/settle.js:17:12)
    at IncomingMessage.handleStreamEnd (/srv/api/node_modules/axios/lib/adapters/http.js:332:11)

The code calling external verification service is synchronous and not using timeouts:

// webhook.controller.ts
const res = await axios.post(VERIFY_URL, payload);
if (res.status !== 200) throw new Error('verification failed');

Refactor to use a typed HttpClient with timeout/retries and proper error translation (throw ApiError with status 502 on upstream failure). Add tests to simulate upstream 4xx/5xx and ensure webhook 200/400/502 mapping.

Long: The GraphQL layer exposes internal fields accidentally. Example schema resolver code:

// resolvers.ts
const User = {
  password: (parent) => parent.password,
  email: (parent) => parent.email,
};

Network log shows queries returning password hashes. Need to refactor to shape returned data explicitly and use DataLoader for N+1 protection. Proposed changes:
- Remove password resolver and map User type to a sanitized DTO in service layer
- Use typed GraphQL context with currentUser: User | null
- Introduce DataLoader for batch userById calls
Provide TypeScript code sample for sanitizedUser mapping and DataLoader setup.

Long: We're seeing a memory leak in the API when processing large CSV imports. Heap snapshot points to open streams not being closed and large arrays retained by the parser.

Relevant code:

// import.service.ts
async function importCsv(filePath: string) {
  const data = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
  const rows = csvParse(data);
  for (const r of rows) { await processRow(r); }
}

This reads the entire file into memory. Refactor to use streaming parser:

const stream = fs.createReadStream(filePath).pipe(csv());
for await (const row of stream) { await processRow(row); }

Also ensure processRow does not keep references and that errors are handled per-row (log and continue). Add integration test for large file processing and update types for row shape.

Long: The TypeScript API's DTOs are inconsistent. Example error in the build:

src/controllers/order.controller.ts:45:12 - error TS2322: Type '{ id: string; amount: number; payment: any; }' is not assignable to type 'OrderDto'.
  Property 'status' is missing in type.

Controller code:

const dto: OrderDto = { id, amount, payment };
return res.json(dto);

OrderDto:
interface OrderDto { id: string; amount: number; status: 'pending' | 'paid' | 'failed'; payment?: PaymentDto }

Refactor controllers to use factory functions createOrderDto(entity) that fills defaults, and make DTO construction consistent. Add unit tests asserting types and runtime shapes, and enable strictNullChecks in tsconfig.

Long: There are race conditions in token refresh logic. Trace:

Sequence: Client A and B both call refresh endpoint before token is persisted. Both get new tokens; old token may still be valid causing duplication. Code:

async function refresh(req) {
  const { refreshToken } = req.body;
  const payload = jwt.verify(refreshToken, REFRESH_KEY);
  const user = await db.User.findOne({ id: payload.sub });
  user.refreshToken = generateNewToken();
  await user.save();
  return res.json({ token: createAccessToken(user) });
}

Need to refactor to use DB-level single-row updates (UPDATE ... WHERE refreshToken = ?) returning affected rows or use optimistic locking (version column) to avoid concurrent refresh anomalies. Update tests to simulate parallel refresh calls and assert single valid refresh token.

Long: TypeORM entity relations cause eager loads across many endpoints. Example log shows slow queries:

SELECT * FROM user INNER JOIN profile ON ... INNER JOIN posts ON ...

Entities:

@Entity()
export class User { @OneToOne(() => Profile, profile => profile.user, { eager: true }) profile: Profile; @OneToMany(() => Post, post => post.author, { eager: true }) posts: Post[] }

Refactor to remove eager: true and explicitly join relations in queries where needed. Add repository methods like findUserWithProfile(id) that use createQueryBuilder and select only necessary fields. Update services and controllers accordingly. Add performance tests demonstrating reduced query time.

Long: The API throws this runtime error during bulk updates:

UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning: TypeError: queryRunner.manager.save is not a function
    at BulkUpdateService.updateAll (/app/src/services/bulkUpdate.ts:58:22)

Code snippet:

async updateAll(items: UpdateItem[]) {
  const queryRunner = this.connection.createQueryRunner();
  await queryRunner.startTransaction();
  try {
    for (const it of items) {
      await queryRunner.manager.save('User', it);
    }
    await queryRunner.commitTransaction();
  } catch (e) { await queryRunner.rollbackTransaction(); throw e; }
}

Problem: save second parameter expects entity object or options; also using string table name is wrong with TypeORM manager. Refactor to use repository methods from queryRunner.manager.getRepository(User).save(entity) and batch save to reduce round trips. Add transactional tests and fix typing so TypeScript enforces entity types.

Long: Tests failing after migration to strict mode with errors like:

TS2345: Argument of type 'unknown' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.

Example problematic code in auth middleware:

const token = req.headers['authorization'];
const payload = jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET);

Because headers can be string|string[]|undefined, and jwt.verify expects string. Refactor middleware to coerce and validate header types, add runtime checks, and update types: const token = Array.isArray(auth) ? auth[0] : auth; if (!token) throw new ApiError(401,'No token'); Also add type guards for unknown results from jwt.verify using zod or typed assertion, and update tests for auth middleware.

Long: We have an endpoint that serializes large objects and causes blocking serialization time; logs show GC spikes. Example code:

res.json({ users: largeUserArray.map(u => ({ ...u, activities: JSON.parse(u.activities) })) });

activities is stored as JSON string in DB. Refactor to stream the response using JSONStream or paginate results, and move expensive JSON.parse into a background job that enriches a denormalized column for quick reads. Provide code example converting controller to an async generator that yields chunks and uses res.flush() with Express (or switch to Node 18 streams) and types for partial user view.

Long: The existing error handling middleware leaks stack traces to clients in production. Current code:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({ error: err.message, stack: err.stack });
});

Refactor to: log error with logger.error({ err, reqId }), and return { message: 'Internal Server Error', code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR' } unless NODE_ENV === 'development', in which case include stack. Create ApiError with properties status, code, details. Update controllers to throw ApiError instead of generic Error. Provide TypeScript signature for ApiError class and updated middleware code.

Long: The search indexer crashes with the following TypeScript compile error after refactor:

src/indexer.ts(112,16): error TS2322: Type 'Promise<unknown>' is not assignable to type 'Promise<void>'.

Original function:

async function indexAll() {
  const tasks = items.map(async (i) => { await indexItem(i); });
  return Promise.all(tasks);
}

But callers expect Promise<void>. Refactor indexAll to await Promise.all(tasks) and explicit return void. Also add concurrency limit with p-limit to avoid overloading search engine. Provide TypeScript code:

export async function indexAll(items: Item[]): Promise<void> {
  const limit = pLimit(10);
  await Promise.all(items.map(i => limit(() => indexItem(i))));
}

Add unit tests for empty items, errors in indexItem, and ensure errors are aggregated and cause proper logs.

Long: We need to migrate from a hand-rolled SQL builder to Prisma client for type-safe DB access. Current code snippet:

// raw sql
const rows = await db.query(`SELECT u.id, u.email FROM users u WHERE u.active = $1`, [true]);

Proposed change: Replace with Prisma:

const users = await prisma.user.findMany({ where: { active: true }, select: { id: true, email: true } });

Task details: create a thin UserRepository that wraps Prisma, migrate a set of endpoints (users list, get by id, update email), add mapping to UserDto, update transactional flows to use prisma.$transaction, and add migration notes. Ensure to keep raw SQL only for complex reports and type them with zod.

Long: There's a high CPU loop in notification dispatcher. Profiler points to recursive Promise chains and unbounded setTimeout. Code:

async function dispatch() {
  while (true) {
    const notifications = await queue.fetch();
    notifications.forEach(async (n) => { await send(n); });
    await sleep(100);
  }
}

Problems: notifications.forEach with async doesn't await, and while (true) will run forever even when queue empty. Refactor to use for (const n of notifications) await send(n) or use Promise.all with concurrency control, and add graceful shutdown signal handling. Provide updated TypeScript implementation using AbortController and p-limit.

Long: The health-check endpoint frequently reports 'database disconnected' even though DB is fine. Logs:

Error: connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:5432
    at TCPConnectWrap.afterConnect [as oncomplete] (node:net:1191:16)

Current health-check implementation does a full schema query synchronously:

app.get('/health', async (req,res) => {
  await db.query('SELECT 1');
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});

Refactor health check to use cached DB pool ping with timeout (e.g., attempt connect with 1s timeout) and make the endpoint non-blocking: return degraded status if DB slow but still accept requests. Add readiness vs liveness endpoints: /live returns 200 if process running; /ready verifies DB and migrations applied. Provide code samples and tests that simulate DB slow response.

Long: We need to standardize the error response schema. Currently some endpoints return { message: string } others { error: { code, message } } and integration tests fail. Propose a single schema:

{ success: boolean, data?: T, error?: { code: string, message: string, details?: any } }

Refactor controllers and error middleware to use ApiResponse<T> generic. Provide TypeScript types and example controller refactor:

function sendSuccess<T>(res: Response, data: T) { res.json({ success: true, data }); }
function sendError(res: Response, err: ApiError) { res.status(err.status).json({ success: false, error: { code: err.code, message: err.message } }); }

Update tests and client consumption notes.

Long: The TypeScript service returns different timestamp formats across endpoints (ISO strings vs epoch ms). This causes frontend bugs. Example:

// orders.controller.ts
return res.json({ createdAt: order.createdAt.getTime() });
// users.controller.ts
return res.json({ createdAt: user.createdAt.toISOString() });

Standardize to ISO 8601 strings and create a serializer helper formatDate(date: Date | string | number): string. Refactor all controllers to use it and update OpenAPI docs to mark date-time format. Add unit tests ensuring consistent formatting and backwards-compatible migration notes for clients.

Long: We have inconsistent transaction management when calling external services within DB transactions. Example bug: We update DB then call external payment API; if payment fails we rollback DB but cannot rollback side-effect in payment provider. Code snippet:

await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.order.update(...);
  const res = await paymentClient.charge(...); // external
  if (!res.success) throw new Error('payment failed');
});

Refactor to follow the saga pattern: reserve inventory and create a 'pending' order in transaction, commit, then perform payment; if payment fails, mark order as failed and trigger compensating actions. Add clear documentation and provide TypeScript example of saga orchestration using a state machine and audit logs for retries.

Long: The frontend team reports that the API returns 204 responses with bodies in some endpoints, causing fetch to throw when parsing JSON. Example controller:

if (!result) return res.status(204).json({ message: 'No content' });

HTTP 204 must not include a body. Refactor controllers to use 404 or 200 with empty list depending on semantics. Audit endpoints for misuse of 204 and update tests and client docs. Provide a script to scan codebase for res.status(204).json occurrences and propose fixes.

Long: Refactor code to eliminate direct use of any in repository layer. Example problematic function:

async function findById(id: string): Promise<any> { return db.findOne({ id }); }

Replace with typed entity: async function findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> and map DB row to User typed interface. Also add migration notes for sweeping codebase, run TypeScript compiler with noImplicitAny to surface occurrences, and include a codemod suggestion to replace ': Promise<any>' with ': Promise<unknown>' where necessary as intermediate step.

The config loader currently performs synchronous fs.readFile on startup which delays cold start. Change to async read with Promise and keep startup logs until loaded.

Current code (config/loader.js):

const fs = require('fs');
function loadConfig() {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(process.env.CONFIG_PATH || '/etc/myservice/config.json');
  return JSON.parse(content);
}

Problems: blocks event loop during cold start in serverless and containers. Suggested change: use fs.promises.readFile, async/await, and add a fallback timeout and better error handling. Also include a sample async implementation and tests.

Longstanding issue: environment variable parsing coerces 'false' to true because of truthy checks. Fix loader to parse boolean strings properly.

Example bug:
process.env.FEATURE_X = 'false';
if (process.env.FEATURE_X) { enableFeature(); }
// This enables the feature even though it should be disabled.

Proposed: implement a parseEnv helper:

function parseBool(val) {
  if (val === undefined || val === null) return undefined;
  return /^(true|1|yes)$/i.test(String(val));
}

Then use parseBool(process.env.FEATURE_X) and default logic. Add unit tests for values 'true', 'false', '0', '1', '', undefined.

Fix config merging bug: nested objects are shallow-merged causing loss of nested defaults. Implement deep merge and add test cases.

Repro:
default config:
{
  "log": { "format": "json", "level": "info" }
}
override:
{ "log": { "level": "debug" } }

Current result: { "log": { "level": "debug" } } // format lost
Expected: { "log": { "format": "json", "level": "debug" } }

Add deep merge util using lodash.merge or custom recursive merge, include unit tests.

Long: Intermittent NPE when using ApiClient.builder().auth(token).build() — happens only when using custom serializer. Reproduction:
- Configure custom Serializer that returns null for empty object.
- Call client.create(entity)

Stack trace:
java.lang.NullPointerException
    at com.example.sdk.serializer.DefaultMapper.serialize(DefaultMapper.java:45)
    at com.example.sdk.transport.HttpTransport.prepareBody(HttpTransport.java:201)
    at com.example.sdk.client.ApiClient.create(ApiClient.java:88)
    at com.myapp.Main.main(Main.java:25)

Code example:
public class BrokenSerializer implements Serializer {
  @Override
  public byte[] toBytes(Object obj) {
    // bug: returns null for empty POJO
    return null;
  }
}

Request: Validate that Serializer.toBytes never returns null and fail fast with IllegalStateException; add unit tests and update javadoc.

Long: Adding support for reactive backpressure: current Flux-based watchResources floods downstream consumers. Repro/observed behavior:
- Server sends bursts of 10k events
- Client subscriber is overwhelmed, OOME

Current code snippet:
public Flux<ResourceChange> watch() {
  return Flux.create(emitter -> websocket.onMessage(msg -> emitter.next(parse(msg))));
}

Request: Change to use Flux.push or implement buffering with onBackpressureDrop / limitRate, provide config option watchBufferSize and an OverflowStrategy enum. Include example usage showing how to drop oldest events when overwhelmed.

Long: Serialization mismatch for enums: server returns uppercase strings but Enum.valueOf fails for lowercase variants. Example error:
java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: No enum constant com.example.sdk.model.Status.active
    at java.lang.Enum.valueOf(Enum.java:238)

JSON: {"status":"ACTIVE"}
Model: enum Status { active, inactive }

Request: Add @JsonCreator on enums to perform case-insensitive mapping and update existing generated model classes. Include unit tests for ACTIVE, active, Active variations and document behavior.

Long: Thread-safety bug in TokenProvider: when token refresh fails concurrently, multiple refreshes occur causing rate-limit. Code excerpt:
public String getToken() {
  if (token == null || isExpired()) {
    token = refresh(); // no lock
  }
  return token;
}

Observed logs showing multiple refresh calls within milliseconds. Request: Make TokenProvider.refresh atomic with Double-checked locking or use CompletableFuture to coalesce concurrent refresh requests. Add tests simulating 100 concurrent threads calling getToken().

Long: Need to change default pagination to cursor-based paging. Current ListRequest uses pageNumber/pageSize; server now supports cursor tokens. Migration plan:
- Deprecate pageNumber/pageSize in ListRequest
- Add cursor and limit fields
- Provide compatibility layer that translates pageNumber into cursor using an internal mapping where possible

Include code example showing new usage:
ListRequest req = new ListRequest().cursor("abc123").limit(100);
ListResponse resp = client.list(req);
// use resp.nextCursor() to fetch next page

Also update docs and changelog with breaking change notes.

Long: The SDK's generated models include public mutable fields which break encapsulation and cause inconsistent state when used with frameworks like Hibernate. Example model snippet:
public class Account {
  public String id;
  public String name;
}

Request: Regenerate models to use private fields with getters and builders; keep backward compatibility by adding @Deprecated setters for one release cycle and document migration path. Provide code sample for updated usage:
Account account = Account.builder().id("x").name("y").build();

Long: When using Jackson's ObjectMapper with injected modules, the SDK's internal mapper registration clobbers user-registered modules, causing serialization differences. Repro:
ObjectMapper userMapper = new ObjectMapper();
userMapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
Client client = new Client.Builder().mapper(userMapper).build();

But SDK reconfigures mapper and time types are no longer handled, errors on Instant parsing.

Request: Accept user-provided ObjectMapper without mutating global configuration; when changes are required, clone the provided mapper and register modules on the copy. Add unit tests that pass a custom mapper with JavaTimeModule and confirm Instant is serialized/deserialized correctly.

Long: Change error payload parsing to include nested causes. Currently ApiException.message contains only top-level message. Example server response:
{ "error": { "message": "Failed", "cause": { "message": "Validation failed", "field": "email" } } }

SDK currently returns ApiException: "Failed". Request: Parse nested cause into ApiException.getCauseDetails() with structured object; include sample code to access cause field and provide toString override that prints nested causes.

Long: Implement per-request cancellation with CancellationToken. Problem: long-running operations (export, report generation) cannot be cancelled by user once started. Proposal:
- Add CancellationToken class with cancel()
- Accept CancellationToken in Request options
- Internally abort the HTTP call and throw RequestCancelledException

Example usage:
CancellationToken token = new CancellationToken();
Future<Report> f = client.generateReport(req, token);
// later
token.cancel();

Include sample code and unit tests simulating cancellation mid-upload and assert resources are cleaned up.

Long: Failures when deserializing nested generic responses like ApiResponse<List<User>> produce warning about unchecked cast. Example:
ApiResponse<List<User>> resp = client.call(...);
// warning: unchecked conversion

Request: Add typed call method <T> ApiResponse<T> call(Request, Class<T> clazz) and overloads for TypeReference<T> to correctly capture parameterized types. Provide example using new TypeReference<List<User>>() {} and update client usages across codebase.

Long: Add support for IPv6 endpoints. Bug report: Hosts with IPv6 addresses like [2001:db8::1] cause IllegalArgumentException when passed to URL builder. Repro:
client.setHost("[2001:db8::1]");
client.get("/status");

Exception:
java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Illegal character in host name

Request: Update URL building logic to correctly handle IPv6 literals (wrap with brackets) and add tests for URL encoding with ports and IPv6. Ensure compatibility with existing host strings and document behavior.

Long: Provide migration steps and code to change callback-style async API to CompletableFuture-based API. Current pattern:
client.doThing(req, new Callback<Result>() {
  void onSuccess(Result r) {}
  void onFailure(Throwable t) {}
});

We want:
CompletableFuture<Result> doThingAsync(Request)

Request: Add doThingAsync wrappers that adapt existing executor/callback internals to CompletableFuture, deprecate callback API, and include example migration snippet:
// old
client.doThing(req, cb);
// new
client.doThingAsync(req).thenAccept(r -> ...).exceptionally(t -> ...);

Include notes on exception handling differences and provide tests for both paths.

Long: Asset pipeline causing long compile times and large JS bundles. During CI precompilation we see repeated warnings and slow hashing:

rake assets:precompile
Sprockets::DirectiveProcessor: nested require_tree is deprecated
Compiling... 120s
Webpacker compiled packs: application.js (2.1 MB), vendor.js (4.5 MB)

Large vendor bundle includes many unused libraries. Frontend is ERB/JS using Webpacker with Ruby helpers. Proposed changes:
- Tree-shake vendor libs (import only needed modules)
- Split bundles by route with code-splitting (dynamic imports)
- Use gzip and Brotli compression on CDN
- Remove unneeded polyfills for mobile-only targets
- Example: change packs/application.js import to dynamic import for heavy charting lib:

// before
import Chart from 'heavy-chart-lib'

// after
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  if (document.querySelector('.chart')) {
    import('heavy-chart-lib').then(({ default: Chart }) => {
      new Chart(...)
    })
  }
})

Long: The mobile app's onboarding endpoint returns lots of configuration and assets, making initial app launch slow. Endpoint code builds many hashes by invoking external services and reading DB rows serially. Example:

def onboarding
  cfg = FeatureFlagService.get_all_for(user.id)
  banners = Banner.active.map { |b| { html: render_banner(b) } }
  locales = LocaleService.fetch_for(user.country)
  render json: { cfg: cfg, banners: banners, locales: locales }
end

This blocks because FeatureFlagService calls DB on each flag, Banner rendering uses helpers and loads images. Proposed optimizations:
- Precompute onboarding payload per cohort and cache in Redis for 5 minutes
- Return minimal payload and lazy-load banners via separate endpoint
- Use concurrent-ruby futures for independent calls and join results to reduce wall-clock time
- Example change using futures:

f1 = Concurrent::Future.execute { FeatureFlagService.get_all_for(user.id) }
f2 = Concurrent::Future.execute { Banner.active.limit(5).map(&method(:fast_banner_payload)) }
render json: { cfg: f1.value, banners: f2.value }

But prefer caching to avoid double work.

Long: Migration panics with stack trace when applying a multi-statement SQL file. Reproduction steps:
1) Create migration file migrations/20251102010101_add_users.sql with:

-- up
CREATE TABLE users (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX ON users (name);

-- down
DROP TABLE users;

2) Run zig-migrate apply --database-url "postgres://postgres@localhost/db"

Observed panic:

Assertion failed at /home/ci/zig/lib/std/fs.zig:204: index out of bounds (0 > 0)
panic: index out of bounds

Stack trace:
0x400d1b in std.debug.panic
0x40a3ef in std.io.Reader.readUntil (fs.zig)
0x40b4c2 in migrator.parseStatements (migrator.zig:132)
0x40c101 in migrator.applyMigration (migrator.zig:210)

Attached is the migration file. It looks like parseStatements fails when file ends with a newline and trailing comment. Please add robust SQL splitting logic that handles comments, blank lines, and semicolons within dollar-quoted strings.

Also consider adding a code sample for splitting SQL safely, e.g.:

fn splitSQL(db: *std.fs.File) !std.ArrayList([]const u8) {
    var allocator = std.heap.page_allocator;
    var stmts = std.ArrayList([]const u8).init(allocator);
    var buf = std.ArrayList(u8).init(allocator);
    defer stmts.deinit();
    // pseudo-code: stream bytes, track quotes, dollar-quoted tags, escape sequences, and split on top-level semicolons
    return stmts;
}

This needs a test case that includes dollar-quoted functions and multi-line comments.

Long: When running the migration runner in a Kubernetes initContainer, I get inconsistent migration ordering due to locale-influenced file sorting. Repro steps:
1) Create three files:
   migrations/20250101000000_init.sql
   migrations/20250101000001_add_index.sql
   migrations/20250101000002_seed.sql
2) Build Docker image on macOS, run in k8s pod with Locale=ru_RU.UTF-8
3) The migrator applies them out of order: init, seed, index, causing index creation to fail.

Stack trace:

Error: SQLExecutionError { code: "42P07", message: "duplicate_table", sql: "CREATE TABLE users ( ... )" }
Migration apply aborted at migrator.zig:340

The root cause seems to be std.mem.utf8Compare or fs.readdir returning locale-aware ordering. Fix by enforcing bytewise lexicographic ordering on filenames (sort by bytes, not strcoll). Also add tests that set LC_ALL to different locales and assert ordering is stable. Example sorting code to use:

var files = try readDir(allocator, migrations_path);
files.sort(allocator, cmpFn);

fn cmpFn(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    return std.mem.eql(u8, a, b) ? false : std.mem.lexicalLess(u8, a, b);
}

Please also add a diagnostic flag --print-order to the CLI for debugging.

Long: Request to add transactional and non-transactional migration modes, with code examples and tests. Current behavior runs everything in a single transaction; some migrations (like CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY in Postgres) cannot run inside a transaction. Need to allow marking migration files with a header directive indicating transaction_mode = "none"/"auto"/"transactional". Example migration file header:

-- name: 20250401_add_index
-- transaction: none
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_name ON users(name);

Proposed API changes:
- Add MigrationMeta struct with .name, .checksum, .transaction_mode.
- Migrator.applyMigration(meta: MigrationMeta) should branch based on transaction_mode:
    if (meta.transaction_mode == .transactional) { start txn; execute; commit; }
    else if (meta.transaction_mode == .none) { execute statements one by one without wrapping in txn; }

Zig code snippet showing header parsing and usage:

const TransactionMode = enum { Transactional, None, Auto };

fn parseHeader(content: []const u8) TransactionMode {
    if (std.mem.contains(content, "transaction: none")) return .None;
    if (std.mem.contains(content, "transaction: transactional")) return .Transactional;
    return .Auto; // default behavior
}

// in applyMigration
const mode = parseHeader(migration.content);
if (mode == .Transactional) {
    try db.startTransaction();
    defer db.rollbackOnError();
    try executeStatements(db, migration.content);
    try db.commit();
} else {
    try executeStatementsNoTxn(db, migration.content);
}

Add tests for concurrent index creation and ensure migrator honors 'none' mode. Document the header format and place it in README. Also add linter that warns if any migration contains 'CONCURRENTLY' but doesn't declare transaction: none.

Long: Crash when computing migration checksum for very large migration files (>100MB). Repro steps and logs:

1) Create a big migration that seeds large JSON into a table (file size ~120MB)
2) Run migrator check

Runtime panic:

thread 1 panic: allocation exceeded: requested 134217728 bytes
0x40123f std.heap.page_allocator.alloc
0x402aff std.crypto.sha256.init
0x4035d0 migrator.compute_checksum(migrator.zig:56)
0x404001 migrator.loadAllMigrations(migrator.zig:110)

The current checksum implementation reads whole file into memory then hashes. We should stream the file through SHA-256 to avoid OOM. Example Zig code to stream-hash files:

pub fn checksumFile(fs: anytype, path: []const u8, allocator: *std.mem.Allocator) ![32]u8 {
    var file = try fs.openFile(path, .{});
    defer file.close();
    var hasher = std.crypto.sha256.init();
    var buf: [4096]u8 = undefined;
    while (true) {
        const n = try file.read(buf[0..]);
        if (n == 0) break;
        hasher.update(buf[0..n]);
    }
    var digest: [32]u8 = undefined;
    hasher.finish(&digest);
    return digest;
}

Please implement streaming checksums, add tests for large files, and adjust migration table to store hex-formatted checksums. Also add progress logging for large migrations so users know processing is ongoing.

Long: Migration runner reports checksum mismatch and refuses to run, but checksums in DB and local file are identical when printed. Reproduction and logs:

$ zig-migrate verify
ERROR: checksum mismatch for migration 20250303_add_col
  db: d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e
  file: d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e

Stack trace:
panic: checksum mismatch
  at migrator.verify (src/migrator.zig:215)

Investigation: The library stores checksum as hex string but compares binary digest incorrectly due to endianness conversion bug. The code uses std.fmt.bufPrint to format bytes but then trims leading zeros incorrectly, so '00ab' becomes 'ab'. Please fix checksum to use canonical lowercase hex with leading zeros and update tests. Example canonical conversion:

fn toHex(bytes: []const u8) []u8 {
    var out = try allocator.alloc(u8, bytes.len * 2);
    for (i, b) in bytes.iter() {
        out[i*2] = hex[(b >> 4) & 0xF];
        out[i*2 + 1] = hex[b & 0xF];
    }
    return out;
}

Add unit tests with bytes starting with 0x00 and ensure hex output has leading zero. Also add migration table migration to convert older stored checksums to canonical format if needed.

Long: Need to add first-class support for multi-DB deployments (separate DB per tenant). Feature request with code, tests, and migration plan:

Use-case: We need to run the same migration set for 200 tenants. Current library can only point to one database URL. Request to add a TenantMigrator API:

pub const TenantMigrator = struct {
    allocator: *std.mem.Allocator,
    tenant_db_urls: []const []const u8,
    base_migrator: Migrator,

    pub fn runForAll(self) !void {
        for (url) in self.tenant_db_urls {
            var cfg = MigratorConfig{ .database_url = url, ... };
            var m = try Migrator.init(cfg);
            try m.applyPending();
        }
    }
};

Constraints: Should run migrations in parallel with configurable concurrency, but avoid resource exhaustion. Add progress reporting and per-tenant retry/backoff on transient errors. Example CLI: zig-migrate tenants --concurrency 10 --tenants-file tenants.txt

Add tests that spawn multiple ephemeral Postgres via docker compose and verify all tenants are migrated. Also add a dry-run mode to detect schema divergence across tenants.

Long: Migration parsing fails for Zig-embedded SQL migrations due to newline handling. I'm embedding many small .sql files inside a Zig array using @embedFile, then iterating to run them. Example code:

const std = @import("std");

const migrations = []struct { name: []const u8, sql: []const u8 } {
    .{ "0001_init", @embedFile("../migrations/0001_init.sql") },
    .{ "0002_seed", @embedFile("../migrations/0002_seed.sql") },
};

pub fn run(allocator: *std.mem.Allocator) !void {
    var db = try DB.connect(...);
    for (m) in migrations {
        try db.exec(m.sql);
    }
}

Observed problem: some embedded SQL files end up with CRLF (
) and parser's split-on-semicolon logic considers \r as part of statement and fails to recognize ending semicolon. Error:

Error: SQLExecutionError { message: "syntax error at end of input", sql: "INSERT INTO ... VALUES ('x')\r" }

Proposed fix: normalize line endings when embedding or reading files: strip \r characters before splitting statements. Update embedding docs to mention CRLF normalization. Also add a unit test embedding a file with CRLF and ensure migrator handles it. Consider adding helper fn normalizeNewlines(s: []const u8) []const u8 to be applied to embedded content and file reads.

Long: Support for generating SQL migration diffs between two schema snapshots. Request: add command zig-migrate diff --from-schema schema_v1.sql --to-schema schema_v2.sql that outputs ordered SQL statements to transform schema. Use a simple AST-based differ for Postgres with support for tables, columns, indexes, and constraints. Example output:

-- Drop obsolete index
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_old;

-- Add column with default
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now();

Implementation notes: parse CREATE TABLE statements into structs, compute additions/removals/alterations, produce deterministic ordering (create table before adding indexes). Provide Zig snippet showing table representation:

const ColumnDef = struct {
    name: []const u8,
    typ: []const u8,
    nullable: bool,
    default_expr: ?[]const u8,
};

Add tests that feed two large schema files and assert diff applies cleanly to empty Postgres instance. Also provide option to output as a migration file with timestamped name.

Long: Add a helper to convert existing SQL migrations into Zig-embedded migrations for single-binary deployment. Requirements and code example:

- Scan migrations directory
- For each file, generate Zig source file under src/autogen_migrations.zig containing a compile-time array of structs with name and sql
- Names should be valid Zig identifiers (replace '-' '.' with '_')

Example generated snippet:

pub const embedded_migrations = [_]struct { name: []const u8, sql: []const u8 }{
    .{ "20250101_init", \"CREATE TABLE...\" },
    .{ "20250102_seed", \"INSERT INTO...\" },
};

Provide a CLI command zig-migrate embed --out src/autogen_migrations.zig. Ensure proper escaping of quotes and newlines and add a flag --max-line-length to wrap lines. Add tests verifying embeddable output compiles with Zig 0.10. Also add option to include checksums as const bytes arrays so runtime verify() can run without filesystem access.

Long: Add support for running SQL migrations that depend on compiled Zig functions. Use-case: compute deterministic GUIDs or hashes in Zig and inject as parameters into SQL. Proposed API: allow migration to be a Zig function that returns SQL and parameters, compiled into app. Example:

pub fn migration_20250801() !void {
    const sql = fmt("INSERT INTO seeds (id, data) VALUES (%s, %s)", compute_id(), compute_data());
    try db.exec(sql);
}

